# ---------------- main domain lessons (threatlocker-assessment-prep) ----------------
main_lessons = [
L("threatlocker-assessment-prep", "Deny-by-default: the one idea underneath everything",
["Default-allow asks 'is this known-bad?' \u2014 a question that can never be answered completely, because the set of possible malware is infinite. Default-deny asks 'is this explicitly trusted?' \u2014 the set of applications a business needs is finite and knowable.",
"Allowlisting is default-deny applied to execution: only approved applications run. Everything else \u2014 known malware, unknown malware, zero-days, rogue scripts \u2014 is denied without needing a signature or a prior sighting.",
"The operational cost is real: every legitimate new application needs an approval path. Learning Mode, the Approval Center, certificate rules, and the Testing Environment are the machinery that pays that cost without breaking the business.",
"Assessors test whether you can hold both truths at once: default-deny is philosophically stronger AND operationally heavier. Answers that acknowledge the tradeoff score; answers that treat it as magic do not."],
"THE CORE QUESTION\n  default-allow:  'Do I recognize this as bad?'   -> unknown runs\n  default-deny:   'Is this explicitly trusted?' -> unknown is denied\n\n  Finite list wins: every app the business needs\n  vs. infinite list: every malicious file that could exist",
"Memorize this inversion and practice saying it in one breath. It is the ninety-second answer to 'why allowlisting,' the board summary, and the first line of defense when someone calls default-deny 'too strict.'",
["Describing allowlisting as 'antivirus with a list' \u2014 it inverts the trust question; that is the whole point.",
"Forgetting the operational cost \u2014 assessors will ask 'what breaks?' and 'too strict' answers fail.",
"Claiming default-deny stops everything \u2014 approved-but-compromised apps are why Ringfencing exists."],
"Explain default-deny vs default-allow aloud in under 60 seconds, including the operational cost and what pays it."),
L("threatlocker-assessment-prep", "Allowlisting judgment: certificate, hash, path",
["Rule strength, strongest to weakest: CERTIFICATE (publisher) rules trust the signer's identity across versions; HASH rules trust exact bytes and break on every update; PATH rules trust a location and are only as strong as the folder's writability.",
"The version-change trap: a signed application updates, its hash changes, and hash-based rules start blocking a legitimate app. Certificate rules survive version churn \u2014 this is the single most-tested rule-strength judgment.",
"PATH rules in user-writable locations (Downloads, Temp, user profile) are policy holes: anything \u2014 including malware \u2014 can be placed at an approved path. Assessors hunt for exactly this mistake in policy proposals.",
"Scope is the discipline: the narrowest rule that solves the problem. A certificate rule scoped to the updater and its child installer beats a global hash approval; a per-department rule beats a fleet-wide one."],
"RULE STRENGTH LADDER\n  1. certificate  -> survives updates, trusts the signer\n  2. hash        -> exact bytes, breaks every version\n  3. path        -> trusts a location; NEVER in user-writable dirs\n\n  Payroll updater blocked after update?\n  -> certificate rule, scoped to updater + child installer",
"When a scenario shows a blocked legitimate app, walk the ladder: is it signed (certificate rule)? Unsigned but stable (hash)? Only ever a path \u2014 and if so, is the folder locked down? The ladder turns a panic into a procedure.",
["Approving a new hash globally and permanently for one user's request \u2014 scope the exception, time-box it.",
"Using path rules because they are 'easier' \u2014 easier for you and for the attacker.",
"Forgetting to validate after the fix: the good path must work AND an unrelated unsigned binary must still be denied."],
"Take the payroll-updater scenario pattern and write the rule you would create: type, exact scope, and one sentence of justification."),
L("threatlocker-assessment-prep", "Ringfencing: containment after approval",
["Application control answers 'may it run?' Ringfencing answers 'what may it do once running?' \u2014 and most modern attacks live in the gap between those two questions, hijacking trusted applications.",
"Four independent leashes: application-to-application (may it launch other processes?), file (what may it read/write?), registry (what keys may it touch?), network (where may it go?). Each is configured separately.",
"Living-off-the-land is the threat model: attackers prefer Word, PowerShell, and other signed binaries precisely because they inherit trust. Ringfencing assumes the allowed app is already compromised.",
"The executive translation, which assessments test: 'Word is allowed to run, but it may not launch PowerShell, read the credential store, or reach the internet \u2014 so a hijacked Word cannot become ransomware.'"],
"RINGFENCE BASELINE (Office apps)\n  app-to-app: DENY spawning powershell.exe, cmd.exe, wscript.exe\n  file:        DENY writes outside user document folders\n  registry:    DENY writes to Run keys and services\n  network:     ALLOW only update + licensing endpoints",
"Read any Ringfencing scenario as a leash-check: which of the four dimensions did the attacker need, and which leash should have stopped it? The answer is almost always the dimension the policy left at default.",
["Treating Ringfencing as a second allowlist \u2014 it governs behavior of approved apps, not approval itself.",
"Ringfencing only the exotic apps and leaving Office/browser at defaults \u2014 the most-abused apps need the tightest leashes.",
"Forgetting that containment is not cure: a blocked escape attempt is a DETECTION \u2014 investigate the intrusion it revealed."],
"Write a four-line ringfence baseline for a PDF reader, then name which line stops a macro-launched PowerShell."),
L("threatlocker-assessment-prep", "Elevation Control: killing standing admin",
["Every standing local-admin account is pre-staged privilege escalation. Attackers do not hack in; they log in \u2014 with the rights the environment left lying around, including accounts of people who left months ago.",
"Elevation Control replaces standing rights with just-in-time elevation: a SPECIFIC approved application gets admin rights for a SPECIFIC user, time-boxed, with no admin credentials ever exposed.",
"Three properties every elevation decision needs: time-boxed (the grant expires), ringfenced (the elevated process cannot hop to PowerShell or cmd), and logged (every elevated session is audited and reviewable).",
"The service judgment assessors test: a flat 'no' without an alternative fails the usability test; a 'yes' without bounds fails the security test. The professional answer is a bounded yes \u2014 or a no with a path to yes."],
"ELEVATION REQUEST CHECKLIST\n  [ ] specific app identified (not 'make me admin')\n  [ ] binary signed; hash verified against vendor\n  [ ] business justification + approval on record\n  [ ] time-box set (grant expires automatically)\n  [ ] elevated process ringfenced against hopping\n  [ ] session logging enabled",
"Run every elevation scenario through this checklist. Unsigned binary? Stop at line 2. 'Make my team admins'? Fails line 1 \u2014 counter with per-app JIT. The checklist converts pressure into procedure, which is exactly what the assessment measures.",
["Approving elevation for an unsigned binary 'just this once' \u2014 unsigned + elevated is the initial-access jackpot.",
"Granting the PERSON admin instead of the APP elevation \u2014 scope to the application, always.",
"Skipping the log review: unreviewed elevation logs are just disk usage."],
"Take the 'local-admin request at 4:55 PM' pattern and write your counter-offer in two sentences."),
L("threatlocker-assessment-prep", "The Unified Audit habit: evidence before verdict",
["Unified Audit is the single source of truth for 'what was denied and why.' Every triage starts there: the exact binary, the hash, the signer state, the rule that fired, the timestamp.",
"The triage loop: SCOPE (who/what/where) \u2192 OBSERVE (audit evidence) \u2192 HYPOTHESIZE (false positive vs threat vs policy gap) \u2192 ACT (narrowest safe change) \u2192 VALIDATE (good path works, bad path denied) \u2192 DOCUMENT.",
"Three verdicts cover nearly every block: version-change false positive (signed, known vendor, hash changed), unknown-but-requested (unsigned, first-seen \u2014 detonate in the Testing Environment), and policy gap (legitimate need, no rule \u2014 write the narrow rule).",
"Assessors score the ORDER of your thinking: evidence first, verdict second, action third. Leading with the action ('approve it') before the evidence is the most common failure pattern."],
"TRIAGE NOTE (60 seconds)\n  WHAT:    updater blocked, 12 endpoints, 08:04\n  EVIDENCE: signed by known vendor; hash not in catalog\n  VERDICT: version-change false positive\n  ACTION:  scoped certificate rule (updater + child)\n  VALIDATE: install OK; unsigned MSI still denied",
"Practice writing triage notes in this shape until it is automatic. On assessment day, narrating your thinking in this order \u2014 what, evidence, verdict, action, validate \u2014 earns credit even when the final call is debatable.",
["Reading the ticket title instead of the audit \u2014 'payroll down' is urgency, not evidence.",
"Hypothesizing 'attack' or 'false positive' before observing \u2014 the audit usually answers in one screen.",
"Acting without documenting \u2014 the next analyst (or assessor) cannot review what you did not write."],
"Open any scenario in this bootcamp and write its triage note in the five-line shape above before looking at the choices."),
L("threatlocker-assessment-prep", "Triage discipline: the approval workflow",
["The Approval Center is where user requests meet security judgment. Every request arrives as a question \u2014 neither trust nor malice established \u2014 and your job is to turn it into observed evidence before deciding.",
"The decision tree: KNOWN-GOOD (signed, known vendor, catalog match \u2014 approve via proper rule) \u2192 UNKNOWN (first-seen, unsigned \u2014 detonate in the Testing Environment, then decide) \u2192 KNOWN-BAD or policy violation (deny with documented evidence).",
"Urgency never upgrades the rule. 'Finance needs it for month-end' changes the TIMELINE of your triage, not the EVIDENCE STANDARD. Fast triage, same bar.",
"The close-out matters: every decision gets a scoped rule or a documented deny, a validation check, and a note the requester can understand. 'Denied' with no explanation creates shadow IT; 'denied because X, here is the path to yes' creates compliance."],
"APPROVAL DECISION TREE\n  signed + known vendor + catalog match -> approve (proper rule type)\n  unsigned + first-seen             -> Testing Environment first\n  clean sandbox + business need       -> narrow scoped rule\n  suspicious sandbox                -> deny, document evidence\n  urgency high                      -> triage FASTER, same standard",
"When a scenario hands you an approval request, place it on this tree before reading the choices. The tree prunes the tempting wrong answers: global approvals, path rules, 'approve now, verify later' \u2014 none of them survive the tree.",
["Approving by path to clear the queue fast \u2014 speed in triage, never in standards.",
"Denying permanently what should be time-boxed \u2014 'no' should usually be 'not yet, bring evidence.'",
"Forgetting the requester: a deny with no plain-language explanation is a future bypass."],
"Take an 'unknown updater, urgent request' pattern and walk it down the tree aloud, ending with what you tell the requester."),
]
new_lessons.extend(main_lessons)

