# ---------------- elevation-control ----------------
ec = {"id": "elevation-control", "name": "Elevation Control",
"description": "Replacing standing admin with just-in-time, per-app, time-boxed elevation: the request checklist, ringfencing elevated processes, and the audit trail.",
"icon": "\U0001f511", "scenarios": []}
ec_lessons = [
L("elevation-control", "Standing admin is pre-staged privilege escalation",
["Attackers do not hack in; they log in \u2014 with the rights the environment left lying around. Every standing local-admin account, especially orphans of departed employees, is a foothold waiting for a phishing email.",
"Privilege audits consistently find the same pattern: 'temporary' admin grants from years ago, shared admin passwords, and service accounts with interactive logon. Each one is an assessor's favorite finding.",
"The remediation is removal PLUS replacement: strip standing rights, then provide a usable just-in-time elevation path. Removal without replacement drives users to bypasses; replacement without removal leaves the footholds.",
"Auto-removal policies (unused admin accounts purged, unauthorized grants reverted) turn a one-time cleanup into a standing control. Assessors ask 'how do you keep it clean?' \u2014 this is the answer."],
"AUDIT FINDINGS (typical)\n  40 users with standing local admin ('from before rollout')\n  3 admin accounts of departed employees\n  1 shared admin password in the IT wiki\n  Remediation: remove all, replace with JIT per-app elevation",
"When a scenario mentions standing admin, think 'pre-staged escalation' and reach for the full remediation: remove, replace with JIT, purge orphans, ringfence elevated processes, log everything. Partial remediation is the wrong answer.",
["Removing admin rights with no replacement path \u2014 users will route around you.",
"Forgetting orphaned accounts in the audit \u2014 the departed employee's admin is the attacker's favorite.",
"Treating 'no incident yet' as 'no problem' \u2014 assume breach means the foothold counts before it is used."],
"List every admin account you can think of in a typical small company. Which three would an attacker want most?"),
L("elevation-control", "Just-in-time elevation: the five properties",
["SPECIFIC: the grant names an application, not a person. 'John is admin' is standing privilege; 'installer.exe may elevate for John for 60 minutes' is JIT.",
"TIME-BOXED: the grant expires automatically. No manual revocation to forget, no 'temporary' grant lingering for three years.",
"HASH-VERIFIED: the binary's identity is confirmed before elevation \u2014 unsigned code is never elevated, no exceptions, regardless of who asks.",
"RINGFENCED: the elevated process cannot hop to PowerShell, cmd, or other tools. Elevation without containment is just delayed standing admin.",
"LOGGED: every elevated session is recorded and reviewable. Unreviewed logs are disk usage \u2014 the audit trail must be sampled or alerted on."],
"JIT GRANT RECORD\n  app:     installer.exe (sha256: 9f2c..., signed)\n  user:    j.osei | window: 60 min | ticket: CHG-8821\n  ringfence: no child shells | logging: session recorded\n  expiry:  automatic | approver: m.chen",
"Run every elevation scenario against these five properties. A request missing any one of them gets a counter-offer, not a yes \u2014 and the counter-offer names the missing property explicitly.",
["Elevating the PERSON instead of the APP \u2014 scope is the whole discipline.",
"Skipping hash verification 'because we trust the vendor' \u2014 supply-chain attacks are this sentence.",
"Granting without an expiry because 'they might need it later' \u2014 later is a new request."],
"Write a JIT grant record for a fictional printer-driver install. Which property is hardest to satisfy, and why?"),
L("elevation-control", "The elevation request checklist in practice",
["IDENTITY: who is asking, and is the requester who they claim? Elevation for a contractor's personal laptop is a different decision than for a managed workstation.",
"BINARY: signed? hash verified against the vendor? An unsigned driver installer is a full stop \u2014 kernel-adjacent code with no provenance is never elevated.",
"NEED: what specific task requires elevation, and is there a non-elevated path? 'Install the app' sometimes means 'run the installer once' \u2014 which IT can do without granting the user anything.",
"APPROVAL: manager or change-ticket on record. Elevation without a paper trail is a favor, not a control.",
"BOUNDS: time-box, ringfence, logging \u2014 set before the grant, not after."],
"REQUEST TRIAGE\n  who?      j.osei, Finance, managed workstation\n  what?     payroll-driver.exe, SIGNED, hash matches vendor\n  why?      printer rollout, ticket CHG-9902, manager approved\n  bounds?   60 min, no child shells, session recorded\n  verdict:  APPROVE as specified",
"Practice writing this five-line triage until it is reflex. On assessment day, narrating these five lines before your verdict demonstrates the complete elevation judgment in under a minute.",
["Approving from the justification alone ('need it for the demo') without the binary check.",
"Forgetting the non-elevated alternative \u2014 IT running the installer once grants nobody anything.",
"Treating the checklist as paperwork AFTER approving \u2014 the checklist IS the decision."],
"Take the unsigned-driver scenario and run the checklist aloud, stopping at the first failed line with your counter-offer."),
L("elevation-control", "Application hopping: containing the elevated process",
["An elevated process that can spawn PowerShell is standing admin with extra steps. Attackers know this \u2014 'application hopping' from an elevated app to a shell is a standard privilege-escalation path.",
"Ringfence the elevated process explicitly: deny child shells (powershell.exe, cmd.exe, wscript.exe), constrain file writes to the task's directories, and deny network beyond what the task needs.",
"The user experience matters: tell the requester what the elevated app CAN do, not just what it cannot. 'The installer will run with full rights for 60 minutes but cannot launch other programs' sets expectations and prevents the angry callback.",
"Review elevated sessions: sample the logs, alert on elevated processes spawning children, and feed repeat elevation needs back into packaging (an app that needs weekly elevation should be packaged, not repeatedly elevated)."],
"HOPPING SCENARIO\n  granted: installer.exe elevated, 60 min\n  attempt: installer.exe -> powershell.exe  DENIED\n  read: the leash held; the installer did not need a shell.\n  if it HAD needed one: narrow grant for the pinned script.",
"When a scenario shows an elevated app spawning a shell, the question is always: was the shell part of the approved task? If yes, it should have been in the grant (pinned script). If no, the deny is the control working \u2014 and an investigation starts.",
["Elevating without a child-process leash \u2014 the most common elevation policy hole.",
"Allowing 'the installer needs PowerShell' as a blanket statement \u2014 which script, which arguments? Pin them.",
"Never reviewing elevation logs \u2014 repeat elevations are packaging candidates, and anomalies are detections."],
"An installer needs to run one PowerShell script. Write the narrow grant: which script, what bounds, what logging?"),
]
for _l in ec_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
ec_scenarios = [
S("tlap-ec-01", "elevation-control", "The shared admin password in the wiki",
"Your first-week audit finds the IT wiki page 'Passwords' lists a shared local-admin password 'for emergencies,' last changed in 2021, known to at least 15 current and former staff. No incidents \u2014 yet. The assessor asks for your remediation plan.",
["standing-privilege", "shared-credentials", "remediation"],
[["The exposure", "A 5-year-old shared admin password known to former employees: every departed staff member retains domain-wide foothold capability."],
["Assume breach", "Treat the credential as compromised \u2014 you cannot prove it is not. The question is not 'was it used' but 'what did it expose.'"],
["The replacement", "Break-glass accounts (individual, vaulted, alerted on use) replace shared passwords; JIT elevation replaces routine need."]],
"What is your plan?",
["Change the shared password and keep it in the wiki \u2014 it is needed for emergencies",
"Immediate: rotate the credential everywhere and remove it from the wiki; audit where it was used (log review for the account); replace with individual vaulted break-glass accounts that alert on use, plus JIT elevation for routine needs \u2014 and document the incident as a credential-exposure response",
"Delete the wiki page; the password is probably fine since nothing happened",
"Keep the password but tell everyone not to share it further"],
1,
"'No incident yet' \u2014 is that evidence of safety, or absence of detection? Assume breach and answer again.",
"Correct is B: it treats the exposure as a compromise (assume breach), rotates, audits historical use, and replaces the shared secret with the two proper mechanisms (vaulted break-glass + JIT). A is wrong: changing a shared password that 15+ people know just creates a new shared secret with the same flaw. C is wrong: deleting the page does not rotate the credential or investigate its use. D is wrong: asking people not to share a password that is already shared is not a control.",
7),
S("tlap-ec-02", "elevation-control", "The developer who 'needs admin for Docker'",
"A developer requests permanent local admin 'to run containers for development.' The ticket has manager approval. The developer is frustrated: 'every other company just gives devs admin.' The assessor tests your service-plus-security judgment.",
["least-privilege", "developer-workflows", "service-judgment"],
[["The real need", "Container development needs specific capabilities (virtualization, the container runtime service) \u2014 not interactive admin rights over the whole machine."],
["The risk of yes", "Permanent admin on a machine that pulls arbitrary container images from the internet: the highest-risk user profile with the broadest rights."],
["The middle path", "Grant the specific capabilities (Hyper-V/Virtualization rights, container service control), pre-approve the toolchain via allowlist, and offer JIT elevation for the rare true-admin task \u2014 documented, bounded."]],
"How do you respond?",
["Grant permanent admin \u2014 the manager approved it and developers need it",
"Refuse outright: developers never get admin, no exceptions",
"Decompose the need: grant the specific capabilities the container workflow requires (virtualization rights, runtime service), allowlist the toolchain, and provide JIT elevation for genuine one-off admin tasks \u2014 explain the decomposition to the developer so the 'no' comes with a working 'yes'",
"Tell the developer to use a personal machine for development"],
1,
"What does the developer actually need \u2014 'admin,' or three specific capabilities? Answer that and the ticket solves itself.",
"Correct is C: it decomposes 'admin' into actual needs, grants those specifically, keeps JIT for the remainder, and \u2014 the service signal \u2014 explains the decomposition so the developer understands. A is wrong: manager approval does not convert standing admin into a good idea. B is wrong: a flat no with no alternative fails the service test when a scoped path exists. D is wrong: pushing work to unmanaged machines is bypass-as-policy.",
6),
S("tlap-ec-03", "elevation-control", "The elevation log nobody reads",
"Policy requires every elevated session to be logged. Audit finds 300 elevated sessions last quarter \u2014 and zero reviews. One session shows installer.exe spawning powershell.exe with an encoded command; the ringfence allowed it because the grant had no child-process leash. The assessor asks for findings.",
["audit-trail", "monitoring", "hopping"],
[["Finding 1", "Unreviewed logs: the control generates evidence nobody examines. 300 sessions, zero reviews = the audit trail is decorative."],
["Finding 2", "The encoded PowerShell under an unleashed elevation grant: either a legitimate installer step that should have been pinned in the grant, or an actual hopping incident \u2014 UNKNOWN, because nobody looked."],
["The fix", "Leash elevated processes by default (deny child shells unless explicitly granted for a pinned script); sample or alert on elevated sessions; feed the finding back into grant templates."]],
"What are your findings and recommendations?",
["No issue \u2014 the elevation was approved, so the session was legitimate",
"Two findings: (1) elevation logs are collected but never reviewed \u2014 implement sampling/alerting on elevated sessions; (2) the grant lacked a child-process leash, allowing an encoded PowerShell under elevation \u2014 investigate that session now (pinned script or hopping incident?), and make 'deny child shells by default' part of the grant template",
"Disable elevation logging to reduce noise",
"Revoke all elevation grants and start over"],
1,
"An approved grant plus an unreviewed log plus an encoded shell. Which of those three is the finding?",
"Correct is B: it identifies BOTH systemic findings (unreviewed logs, unleashed grant template), demands immediate investigation of the specific session, and repairs the template. A is wrong: 'approved' describes the grant, not the session's behavior \u2014 the encoded shell is precisely what review exists to catch. C is wrong: deleting telemetry to reduce noise is blinding yourself. D is wrong: mass revocation without diagnosis punishes every user for a monitoring failure.",
7),
S("tlap-ec-04", "elevation-control", "The contractor's laptop",
"A contractor needs to install a diagnostic tool on their OWN laptop to troubleshoot your site's equipment. They request local admin on the laptop plus VPN access 'to make it easier.' The tool is signed by a vendor you recognize. The assessor asks how you scope this.",
["third-party", "unmanaged-devices", "scoping"],
[["What you control", "Almost nothing: it is their hardware, their OS state, their other software. Your trust boundary ends at your network and your equipment."],
["The tool", "Signed by a known vendor \u2014 the binary is trustworthy; the ENVIRONMENT is not."],
["The scoping", "No VPN to the flat network: provide the specific access the diagnostic needs (a jump host, a scoped segment, a time window). The laptop never gets standing access; the contractor never gets your admin."]],
"How do you handle the request?",
["Grant VPN access and let them install the tool \u2014 the vendor is known",
"Deny everything; contractors use only company equipment",
"Scope it: verify the tool's hash against the vendor, provide access only to what the diagnostic needs (jump host or scoped segment, time-boxed), no VPN to the flat network, no admin on your systems \u2014 the binary is trusted, the unmanaged environment is not, so trust is granted narrowly and temporarily",
"Give them a domain account with local admin on a spare machine"],
1,
"Separate the two trust questions: the TOOL and the ENVIRONMENT. Which one can you verify?",
"Correct is C: it separates binary trust (verifiable, granted) from environment trust (unverifiable, never granted broadly), and scopes network access to the diagnostic need via a jump host with a time-box. A is wrong: VPN to the flat network from an unmanaged laptop extends your trust boundary to their OS state. B is wrong: when company equipment is not an option, scoped access is the professional answer \u2014 flat denial without alternatives fails the service test. D is wrong: a domain account with admin for a contractor is standing privilege for a temporary need.",
6),
S("tlap-ec-05", "elevation-control", "The service account with interactive logon",
"Audit finds svc-backup: a service account with Domain Admin rights AND interactive logon allowed, password unchanged in 4 years, used on 12 servers. 'It needs admin for the backups,' says the sysadmin. The assessor asks for your assessment.",
["service-accounts", "least-privilege", "standing-privilege"],
[["The exposure", "Domain Admin + interactive logon + 4-year-old password + 12 servers: compromise of one server yields the keys to the domain, and the credential is effectively public to anyone who ever saw it."],
["The real need", "Backups need specific rights (backup operator, service logon) \u2014 not Domain Admin, not interactive logon. 'Needs admin' usually means 'was given admin once and nobody scoped it.'"],
["The fix", "Decompose: managed service account or group-managed service account (automatic password rotation), minimum rights (backup operator), deny interactive logon, scope to the servers that need it."]],
"What is your assessment and remediation?",
["Leave it \u2014 backups are critical and it works",
"Finding: a standing Domain Admin service account with interactive logon and a 4-year-old password is a domain-compromise waiting for one server breach \u2014 remediate by decomposing to least privilege (backup-operator rights, managed service account with automatic rotation, deny interactive logon, scoped to required servers), with a tested rollback",
"Change the password and keep everything else",
"Disable the account immediately with no replacement"],
1,
"'It needs admin for the backups' \u2014 which admin rights, exactly? Make them name it.",
"Correct is B: it names the full exposure chain, decomposes the need to actual rights, uses managed service accounts for rotation, and demands a tested rollback (backups are critical \u2014 the fix must not break them). A is wrong: 'it works' is not a risk assessment. C is wrong: a new password on a Domain Admin with interactive logon is a fresh coat on a rotten structure. D is wrong: disabling the backup account without a replacement breaks backups \u2014 the cure must not kill the patient.",
7),
S("tlap-ec-06", "elevation-control", "The repeat elevation: package it",
"Helpdesk data: the same CAD plugin requires JIT elevation on 40 workstations every month for its update. Each elevation is properly approved, bounded, and logged \u2014 40 tickets a month, every month. The assessor asks what the data is telling you.",
["operational-maturity", "packaging", "metrics"],
[["What the data says", "A recurring, predictable, identical elevation need: the textbook candidate for packaging. The process works \u2014 and its success is now the bottleneck."],
["The maturity move", "Package the update (deploy via management tooling with system rights) or get the vendor's updater allowlisted with a certificate rule \u2014 eliminate the need instead of efficiently serving it."],
["The metric", "Elevation frequency per application: the top of that list is the packaging backlog, prioritized by ticket volume."]],
"What do you recommend?",
["Keep the JIT process \u2014 it is working correctly and securely",
"Recognize the pattern: a monthly identical elevation across 40 machines is a packaging candidate \u2014 deploy the update via management tooling or a certificate-ruled updater instead, and track elevation frequency per app as the packaging backlog metric",
"Grant the CAD users permanent admin to eliminate the tickets",
"Batch all 40 elevations into one monthly change window"],
1,
"The process is working. So why is the ticket count the problem? What does a repeated identical need indicate?",
"Correct is B: it reads the metric correctly (repetition = packaging candidate), eliminates the need structurally, and creates the ongoing metric. A is wrong: 'working' at 40 tickets/month is working expensively \u2014 maturity means eliminating toil, not admiring it. C is wrong: permanent admin to save tickets is the classic toil-for-risk trade, and a terrible one. D is wrong: batching makes the toil efficient without removing it.",
5),
]
ec["scenarios"] = ec_scenarios
new_domains.append(ec)

# ---------------- network-control ----------------
nc = {"id": "network-control", "name": "Network Control",
"description": "Deny-by-default for the wire: host firewall policy, egress allowlisting by destination, and why approving the app never approved everywhere it can go.",
"icon": "\U0001f310", "scenarios": []}
nc_lessons = [
L("network-control", "The host firewall as a Zero Trust control",
["Network Control is the host-based firewall under deny-by-default management: every inbound and outbound flow is allowed only by explicit rule. The endpoint's network posture becomes policy, not accident.",
"Default posture: inbound deny-all except explicitly required services; outbound allowlisted per application AND destination. 'The app is approved' never implies 'the app may reach anywhere.'",
"Rules are structured: application identity + direction + protocol + port + destination scope. A rule missing any element is a broader grant than intended \u2014 audit rules for completeness the way you audit allowlist rules for scope.",
"The firewall log is a detection source: denied egress attempts from approved apps are the network equivalent of ringfence denies \u2014 investigate the behavior, not just the block."],
"FIREWALL RULE ANATOMY\n  app: chrome.exe | dir: out | proto: tcp/443\n  dest: *.update.vendor.com (NOT 'any')\n  why: browser updates | ticket: CHG-####\n  review: quarterly",
"Write every rule in this shape. The 'NOT any' is the discipline: destination-scoped egress is what separates Network Control from a decorative firewall.",
["Leaving outbound as allow-all 'because the allowlist handles execution' \u2014 execution control and egress control are different questions.",
"Writing rules without the business justification \u2014 unreviewable rules accumulate into an unmanageable policy.",
"Forgetting inbound: servers with 'any-any inbound for testing' are the assessor's favorite finding."],
"Write three firewall rules in the anatomy shape above: a browser, an updater, and a backup agent. Compare their destination scopes."),
L("network-control", "Egress allowlisting: scope the destination",
["The core lesson of every egress incident: the policy approved the APPLICATION, not its destinations. Egress allowlisting scopes WHERE an approved app may talk.",
"Destination scoping tools: specific IPs/ports for fixed infrastructure, domain-scoped rules for vendor CDNs, and dynamic ACLs where the vendor publishes endpoint lists. Match the tool to the destination's stability.",
"The beacon test: if an approved app started beaconing to a foreign ASN every 90 seconds, would your rules allow it? If the rule says 'backup app: any:443,' the answer is yes \u2014 and the rule is the vulnerability.",
"Tightening egress is staged like any rollout: Monitor Only first to catalog legitimate destinations, then enforce per application class, with an approval path for new destinations. Surprise enforcement breaks the business."],
"EGRESS REVIEW\n  rule: 'backup-agent: tcp/443 -> ANY'\n  test: beacon to foreign ASN:443 -> ALLOWED (bad)\n  fix:  'backup-agent: tcp/443 -> vendor endpoints only'\n  test: beacon to foreign ASN:443 -> DENIED (good)\n         backup to vendor:443        -> ALLOWED (good)",
"Run the beacon test on every egress rule you review. The rule must deny the malicious destination AND allow the legitimate one \u2014 that pair is the validation, the same double-validation pattern as allowlist fixes.",
["Approving destinations by IP for CDN-fronted services \u2014 the IPs rotate; use domain scoping or the vendor's published list.",
"Forgetting that DNS itself needs a rule \u2014 egress-scoped apps still need to resolve names; scope DNS to approved resolvers.",
"Enforcing egress fleet-wide on day one \u2014 catalog in Monitor Only first, or Monday breaks."],
"Take the 'beacon nobody approved' scenario and write the corrected rule plus the two validation tests."),
L("network-control", "Inbound: the smaller the surface, the better",
["Inbound deny-by-default: no listening service is reachable unless a rule explicitly permits it, scoped by source. The question per service: who legitimately connects, from where?",
"Workstations typically need ZERO inbound allows. Servers need few, each scoped: 'RDP from the admin jump-host range only' \u2014 never 'RDP from any.'",
"Stale inbound rules are a top assessment finding: the 'temporary' allow for last year's migration, the vendor support rule nobody removed. Every inbound rule needs an owner and a review date.",
"Validate inbound posture empirically: port-scan your own endpoints (with authorization) and compare open ports to the rule set. The scan is the truth; the policy is the claim."],
"INBOUND HYGIENE\n  workstation: inbound DENY ALL (no exceptions without ticket)\n  server:    per-service allows, source-scoped\n  every rule: owner + review date + ticket\n  validate:  scan and compare, quarterly",
"When a scenario involves inbound access, start from deny-all and make the requester justify each opening \u2014 source, service, duration. 'We need RDP' is the start of the conversation, not the end.",
["'Temporary' inbound allows without expiry \u2014 the migration ended two years ago; the rule did not.",
"Scoping by port but not by source \u2014 'RDP open' and 'RDP open to the admin range' are different universes of risk.",
"Forgetting IPv6 \u2014 dual-stack endpoints need both families in the policy."],
"Audit three invented inbound rules. For each: is the source scoped, is there an owner, is there a review date?"),
L("network-control", "Network denies as detections",
["A denied egress attempt from an approved application is an event, not noise: the app tried to go somewhere the policy forbids. The question is always WHY \u2014 misconfiguration, vendor change, or compromise.",
"The triage: check the destination (known vendor endpoint? new CDN node? foreign ASN?), check the timing (correlates with an update? 3 AM with no user?), check the app's recent behavior (first time or pattern?).",
"Correlate with the other controls: an egress deny PLUS a ringfence deny from the same process is a much stronger signal than either alone. Defense in depth means the signals combine.",
"Feed the findings back into policy: legitimate new destinations get scoped rules (with the vendor's published list as evidence); malicious ones get blocked and investigated. Every deny should improve either the policy or the investigation."],
"DENY TRIAGE\n  what:  chrome.exe -> 185.220.x.x:443 DENIED, 03:00\n  dest:  no DNS history, foreign ASN -> suspicious\n  timing: no user logged on -> investigate, don't shrug\n  correl: ringfence registry deny same host -> escalate\n  action: isolate + hunt, then policy decision",
"Practice this triage shape on every network scenario. The destination reputation, the timing, and the correlation each add a vote \u2014 and 'no user logged on at 3 AM' is a heavy vote.",
["Treating denied-egress logs as firewall noise \u2014 they are the network's detection feed.",
"Investigating the destination without the timing \u2014 'weird IP' at 3 AM with no user is a different case than at 10 AM.",
"Updating the rule without investigating the cause \u2014 the policy fix and the investigation are both required."],
"Write a deny triage for: backup agent to a new IP at 2 PM Tuesday, vendor announced a CDN migration Monday. Verdict?"),
]
for _l in nc_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
nc_scenarios = [
S("tlap-nc-01", "network-control", "The vendor's CDN migration",
"Monday: the vendor announces a CDN migration \u2014 new IP ranges, same service. Tuesday 2 PM: your backup agent's egress denies spike; the agent cannot reach the new endpoints. The backup window is tonight. The assessor asks for your response.",
["change-management", "egress-scoping", "vendor-coordination"],
[["What happened", "Legitimate destination change: the vendor moved, the policy did not. The denies are correct enforcement of a now-stale rule."],
["The evidence", "Vendor's published migration notice with the new ranges + denies correlating exactly to the migration window = misconfiguration, not compromise."],
["The fix", "Update the scoped rule to the vendor's published new ranges (evidence on file), validate backup traffic flows AND a non-vendor destination is still denied, document the change."]],
"What do you do?",
["Add a temporary 'any-any' egress rule until the backups complete",
"Verify the vendor's published new ranges, update the scoped egress rule to those ranges with the notice as evidence, validate (backup flows, non-vendor still denied), document as a change \u2014 and add 'vendor endpoint-change monitoring' to the recurring checklist",
"Tell the vendor to migrate back",
"Disable Network Control for the backup servers tonight"],
1,
"The vendor moved and told you. What is the evidence-based response \u2014 and what process prevents the next surprise?",
"Correct is B: it verifies against the vendor's published source (not rumor), keeps the rule scoped (no any-any), double-validates, documents, and \u2014 the maturity signal \u2014 adds endpoint-change monitoring so the next migration is anticipated. A is wrong: any-any 'temporarily' is the bypass that outlives its timer. C is wrong: the vendor will not un-migrate for your firewall. D is wrong: disabling the control for the backup servers trades tonight's backup for an unmonitored window.",
6),
S("tlap-nc-02", "network-control", "RDP open to the world",
"Assessment walkthrough of a server: Network Control shows an inbound rule \u2014 RDP (3389) from ANY, added 'for the migration' 18 months ago. The server holds finance data. The assessor asks for your finding and remediation.",
["inbound-hygiene", "stale-rules", "risk-prioritization"],
[["The finding", "Internet-reachable RDP on a finance server via an 18-month-old 'temporary' rule: one of the highest-risk misconfigurations in the environment."],
["Why it matters", "RDP from any is a brute-force and vulnerability target; on a finance server it is a breach-in-waiting with compliance implications."],
["The remediation", "Immediate: scope to the admin jump-host range (or remove if unused). Then: audit ALL inbound rules for owner, review date, and source scope \u2014 the stale rule is a symptom of a missing review process."]],
"What is your finding and fix?",
["It is fine \u2014 the migration needed it and RDP requires a password",
"Finding: stale any-source RDP inbound on a finance server \u2014 immediate remediation (scope to jump-host range or remove), then a full inbound-rule audit for ownership, review dates, and source scoping, since the stale rule indicates a process gap",
"Remove RDP entirely; nobody needs it",
"Change the RDP port to something obscure"],
1,
"Eighteen months, any source, finance data. What does the AGE of the rule tell you about the PROCESS?",
"Correct is B: it treats the rule as both an immediate risk (remediate now) and a process symptom (audit all inbound rules for the missing review discipline). A is wrong: 'requires a password' ignores brute-forcing, credential stuffing, and RDP vulnerabilities \u2014 and 'the migration needed it' expired 18 months ago. C is wrong: removing RDP entirely may break legitimate administration; scope it, don't amputate. D is wrong: port obscurity is not a control.",
6),
S("tlap-nc-03", "network-control", "The app that needs 'the internet'",
"A department requests a firewall exception: 'our analytics tool needs internet access.' No destinations specified, no ports, no justification beyond 'it syncs to the cloud.' The assessor asks how you handle the request.",
["requirements-gathering", "egress-scoping", "service-judgment"],
[["What is missing", "Everything that makes a rule: which destinations, which ports/protocols, which endpoints of the vendor's service, what data flows, what the business need is."],
["The risk of yes-as-asked", "'Internet access' for an app is an unbounded egress grant \u2014 the exact rule the beacon scenario exploited."],
["The professional path", "Ask for the vendor's published endpoint list (every serious SaaS publishes one), scope the rule to those destinations, time-box a review, and document. 'No' to the vague request, 'yes' to the scoped one."]],
"How do you respond?",
["Approve 'internet access' for the app \u2014 the department needs it",
"Deny the request permanently; analytics tools are a data-exfiltration risk",
"Decline the vague request and specify what you need: the vendor's published endpoint list, ports/protocols, and business justification \u2014 then write a destination-scoped rule with a review date. The answer to 'needs the internet' is 'show me where, exactly'",
"Approve it for 30 days and revisit later"],
1,
"'Needs the internet' is not a firewall rule. What information turns it into one?",
"Correct is C: it refuses the unbounded grant while giving the requester the exact path to yes \u2014 vendor endpoint list, ports, justification \u2014 and commits to a scoped rule with review. A is wrong: it grants the beacon-capable rule the whole domain warns about. B is wrong: a permanent deny without a path fails the service test when the need may be legitimate and scopable. D is wrong: 30 days of unbounded egress is 30 days of the exact risk, with a revisit that may not happen.",
5),
S("tlap-nc-04", "network-control", "DNS tunneling suspicion",
"Firewall logs show a workstation making 40,000 DNS queries in an hour to random-looking subdomains of a single domain \u2014 all allowed, because 'DNS is infrastructure.' The assessor asks what you see and what Network Control should have done.",
["dns-security", "detection", "egress-scoping"],
[["The pattern", "High-volume DNS to algorithmically-generated subdomains: the classic DNS tunneling / C2 pattern. Data encoded in queries, answers carrying commands."],
["The policy gap", "'DNS is infrastructure' meant DNS was unscoped: any resolver, any volume, any domain. The firewall allowed the tunnel because the policy never considered DNS as an egress channel."],
["The fix", "Scope DNS: approved resolvers only, alert on volume anomalies and random-subdomain patterns, consider DNS filtering. DNS is infrastructure AND an egress path \u2014 policy must treat it as both."]],
"What is your analysis?",
["DNS is normal traffic; 40,000 queries is probably a misconfigured app \u2014 monitor it",
"Finding: likely DNS tunneling \u2014 the volume plus random-subdomain pattern is the textbook C2 signature; isolate the host and investigate immediately, and fix the policy gap (DNS scoped to approved resolvers with anomaly alerting) because the firewall's blind trust in DNS is what allowed it",
"Block all DNS from the workstation",
"Block the domain at the firewall and close the ticket"],
1,
"Forty thousand queries, random subdomains, one hour. What legitimate application does that?",
"Correct is B: it reads the pattern correctly (volume + entropy = tunneling signature), acts with appropriate urgency (isolate + investigate), and fixes the structural gap (DNS as scoped egress with alerting). A is wrong: 'probably misconfigured' without investigation is the shrug the scenario punishes \u2014 and even a misconfigured app deserves a finding. C is wrong: killing all DNS breaks the host without investigating the active pattern. D is wrong: blocking the domain without isolating/investigating leaves the compromised host and misses lateral scope.",
7),
S("tlap-nc-05", "network-control", "The printer that phones home",
"Egress review: the fleet's printers \u2014 approved devices \u2014 are opening HTTPS connections to the manufacturer's cloud every 15 minutes, plus weekly connections to an analytics domain. The print vendor's documentation mentions 'supply monitoring.' The assessor asks: allow or deny?",
["iot", "data-minimization", "vendor-trust"],
[["What is legitimate", "Supply monitoring (toner levels) to the manufacturer's documented endpoints: a real, documented function."],
["What is questionable", "The analytics domain: usage telemetry to a third party, undocumented in the vendor materials you have. Printers process sensitive documents; their telemetry deserves scrutiny."],
["The scoping", "Allow the documented supply-monitoring endpoints (scoped rule, evidence: vendor docs); investigate the analytics domain \u2014 block until the vendor documents it, and ask what data it carries."]],
"Allow or deny?",
["Allow all of it \u2014 the vendor is legitimate and printers need to phone home",
"Deny all printer egress \u2014 printers have no business on the internet",
"Split the decision: allow the documented supply-monitoring endpoints via a scoped rule (evidence: vendor documentation); block the undocumented analytics domain pending vendor explanation of what data it carries \u2014 documented function gets a rule, undocumented telemetry gets a question",
"Allow it but log everything for a month first"],
1,
"Two destinations, two different evidence levels. Does your answer treat them the same?",
"Correct is C: it splits on the evidence (documented function vs undocumented telemetry), grants the narrow rule for the former, and holds the latter pending answers \u2014 including the key question of what data leaves. A is wrong: 'the vendor is legitimate' does not make undocumented telemetry acceptable. B is wrong: denying documented supply monitoring breaks a real function to avoid thinking. D is wrong: 'log for a month' delays a decision the evidence already supports \u2014 and logging is not a verdict.",
6),
S("tlap-nc-06", "network-control", "Correlating the two denies",
"Same host, same hour: (1) ringfence deny \u2014 winword.exe attempted to spawn powershell.exe; (2) firewall deny \u2014 winword.exe attempted HTTPS to a no-history IP. Either alone might be a misconfiguration. The assessor asks what the PAIR means.",
["correlation", "defense-in-depth", "incident-severity"],
[["Deny 1", "App-to-app escape attempt: the document is trying to break out of Word via PowerShell."],
["Deny 2", "Egress attempt to an unapproved destination: the document is trying to reach an unknown server."],
["The pair", "Two independent controls, two independent dimensions, same process, same hour: this is a coordinated malicious behavior pattern, not two misconfigurations. Defense in depth means the signals combine \u2014 and combined, they describe an active intrusion attempt."]],
"What does the pair tell you, and what do you do?",
["Two misconfigurations; tune both policies to stop the noise",
"The pair is an intrusion pattern: isolate the host immediately, treat as an active incident (not two policy events), investigate the delivery (the document), hunt for the same document elsewhere, and verify via sibling leashes that nothing else escaped \u2014 correlated denies escalate the severity beyond either alone",
"Block the IP and close both tickets",
"Reimage the host quietly to avoid alarming the user"],
1,
"One deny is a policy event. Two denies, two dimensions, one process, one hour \u2014 what is that?",
"Correct is B: it reads the correlation (the whole point of layered controls), escalates to incident handling, and covers the full response (isolate, delivery investigation, lateral hunt, sibling-leash verification). A is wrong: 'tuning the noise' on a correlated intrusion pattern is the catastrophic misread. C is wrong: blocklisting without isolation/investigation leaves the active host. D is wrong: quiet reimaging destroys evidence and skips the hunt \u2014 the delivery may be sitting in other inboxes.",
7),
]
nc["scenarios"] = nc_scenarios
new_domains.append(nc)

# ---------------- storage-control ----------------
sc = {"id": "storage-control", "name": "Storage Control",
"description": "Deny-by-default for removable media and data movement: serial-scoped device approval, encryption and read-only options, and the audit trail that turns blocks into investigations.",
"icon": "\U0001f4be", "scenarios": []}
sc_lessons = [
L("storage-control", "Removable media: the data-exfiltration highway",
["USB drives are the oldest exfiltration path and still among the most effective: no network logs, no firewall, just a pocket-sized copy of the file server. Storage Control brings deny-by-default to the physical port.",
"The default posture: unapproved devices do not connect. Approval is granular: by device SERIAL number (not 'any USB drive'), by user, by file type, and by time window.",
"Monitor Only has a legitimate role \u2014 baselining normal USB use before enforcement \u2014 but it is a phase, not a posture. A device seen in Monitor Only is inventoried; enforcement is the destination.",
"Every file copied, moved, or deleted on controlled storage is audited. The audit trail is what turns a block into an investigation ('what was being copied?') instead of a helpdesk ticket."],
"DEVICE APPROVAL RECORD\n  serial:  8C52-9901-AA | user: k.osei (Finance)\n  scope:  read-only | window: 2026-10-10 to 2026-10-17\n  files:  .xlsx only | encryption: required\n  ticket: CHG-#### | approver: m.chen",
"Write approvals in this shape: serial, user, scope, window, file types, encryption, ticket. Anything missing is a broader grant than intended \u2014 and 'any device, any user, indefinite' is not an approval, it is the absence of control.",
["Approving by device TYPE ('USB drives for Finance') instead of serial \u2014 one lost drive becomes every drive.",
"Leaving Monitor Only on indefinitely \u2014 observation without enforcement is a report, not a control.",
"Forgetting the audit trail in the response \u2014 'what was copied' comes before 'who did it.'"],
"Write an approval record for a contractor who needs to take presentation files offsite for one week."),
L("storage-control", "Granularity: serial, user, type, time",
["SERIAL: the device's unique hardware identity. Approving serial 8C52-9901-AA approves THAT drive \u2014 not the identical-looking drive from the same multipack, and not the attacker's drive.",
"USER: the approval binds to a person. The finance analyst's approved drive does not become the intern's approved drive when handed across the desk.",
"FILE TYPE: constrain what may move. '.xlsx out, .exe never' \u2014 executables have no business leaving on removable media in most environments.",
"TIME: every approval expires. The contractor's week-long grant dies on schedule; renewal is a new decision with fresh evidence. Time-boxing is what keeps 'temporary' honest.",
"Combine all four and you get usable security: the contractor CAN work offsite, on THEIR drive, with PRESENTATION files, THIS week. Granularity is what makes 'yes' safe."],
"GRANULARITY CHECK\n  serial?  8C52-9901-AA (this drive only)\n  user?    k.osei (this person only)\n  type?    .xlsx, .pdf (no executables)\n  time?    7 days, auto-expire\n  result:  a 'yes' with four boundaries",
"When a scenario offers a binary choice (ban all USB vs allow all USB), reach for granularity instead: the four boundaries usually produce a safe yes where both binaries fail. Assessors reward the candidate who finds the third option.",
["Three of four boundaries \u2014 the missing one is the hole; attackers read approvals too.",
"Approving the serial but not the user \u2014 the drive becomes a bearer token.",
"File-type filtering as the ONLY boundary \u2014 renamed executables laugh at extensions; layer it with serial and user."],
"Take 'ban all USB' vs 'allow all USB' and write the granular third option for a marketing team that ships videos to clients weekly."),
L("storage-control", "Encryption and read-only: the safe defaults",
["ENCRYPTION REQUIRED: sensitive data on removable media must be encrypted \u2014 the lost drive is a matter of when, not if. Make encryption a condition of the approval, not a suggestion.",
"READ-ONLY as default for third parties: the contractor can READ the presentation files but cannot WRITE anything back \u2014 which also means nothing malicious comes IN on the return trip.",
"The return trip matters: an approved drive that leaves clean and returns infected is a delivery vector. Read-only for untrusted parties, scan-on-return where the risk warrants it.",
"Document the data classification in the approval: what sensitivity level may travel on this device? 'Confidential' on an unencrypted drive is a finding regardless of whose drive it is."],
"SAFE DEFAULTS\n  third-party device: read-only + time-boxed + serial-scoped\n  sensitive data:     encryption required, no exceptions\n  return trip:        read-only out = nothing malicious back\n  classification:     stated in the approval record",
"Default to these and require justification to loosen them \u2014 not the reverse. 'Why does this need to be writable?' is a better question than 'why not?'",
["Encryption 'recommended' instead of required \u2014 recommendations are not controls.",
"Read-write for vendors 'for convenience' \u2014 the convenience is the attacker's too.",
"Approving the device without stating the data classification \u2014 the approval cannot be reviewed without it."],
"Write the approval for payroll files on an encrypted drive: classification, encryption proof required, and the return-trip rule."),
L("storage-control", "The blocked write: from block to investigation",
["A blocked USB write is a paused exfiltration \u2014 the control worked, and now you have time to assess. The block gives you the gift of time; use it for investigation, not celebration.",
"Assess like an investigator, not a prosecutor: WHAT data (file names, classification), WHAT authorization (approved request on file?), WHAT intent (malicious, careless, or legitimate-but-unapproved?). Evidence first, conclusions later.",
"Three common verdicts: legitimate-but-unapproved (provide the approved path + training), careless violation (document, train, keep the block), malicious (escalate to the incident process \u2014 the block is now evidence).",
"The workflow fix: most 'violations' are really missing workflows \u2014 the user had no approved way to do legitimate work. Provide the approved encrypted transfer method alongside the enforcement, or the control breeds resentment and bypasses."],
"BLOCK TRIAGE\n  what:  2.1 GB, payroll-q3.xlsx (confidential), no request on file\n  assess: data? authorization? intent? (in that order)\n  verdict: careless violation, first offense, cooperative\n  action: document, train, provide approved method, keep block",
"Run this triage on every storage scenario: data, authorization, intent \u2014 in that order. The order matters: 'confidential data, no authorization' already answers the severity question before intent is even considered.",
["Accusation before assessment \u2014 'potential exfiltration' in the ticket poisons the investigation.",
"Keeping the block but providing no approved alternative \u2014 the next attempt will be sneakier.",
"Treating every block as malicious \u2014 the careless majority need training, not termination."],
"Write a block triage for: 500 MB of .mp4 files to an approved serial, within the time window, by the approved user. Verdict?"),
]
for _l in sc_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
sc_scenarios = [
S("tlap-sc-01", "storage-control", "The approved drive that came back infected",
"A contractor's serial-approved, read-WRITE drive returns from a week offsite. The return scan flags a malicious autorun file dated during the trip. The drive was approved for presentation files only. The assessor asks what failed.",
["return-trip", "read-only", "supply-chain"],
[["What was approved", "Serial-scoped, user-bound, presentation files, one week \u2014 but read-WRITE, which the contractor did not need."],
["What happened", "The drive left clean and returned infected: the offsite environment wrote to it. Read-write approval made the return trip a delivery vector."],
["The fix", "Default third-party devices to READ-ONLY (nothing comes back), scan-on-return as a backstop, and require justification for read-write \u2014 the safe default was available and was not used."]],
"What failed, and what is the fix?",
["The contractor \u2014 ban all contractor devices permanently",
"The approval was one boundary short: read-write was granted where read-only sufficed, turning the return trip into a delivery vector \u2014 fix by defaulting third-party devices to read-only, keeping scan-on-return, and containing/assessing the now-quarantined drive before it touches the network",
"The scan failed \u2014 it should have prevented the infection",
"Nothing failed; the scan caught it"],
1,
"The drive left clean and came back dirty. Which granted permission allowed the dirt to board?",
"Correct is B: it identifies the exact missing boundary (read-write vs read-only), fixes the default going forward, and handles the current drive (quarantine + assess before network contact). A is wrong: banning contractors punishes the business for an approval-design flaw. C is wrong: the scan is a backstop, not the control \u2014 the control failure was the writable grant. D is wrong: 'the scan caught it' confuses detection with prevention of the vector.",
6),
S("tlap-sc-02", "storage-control", "The executive's unencrypted drive",
"The CEO's assistant requests approval for the CEO's personal USB drive \u2014 unencrypted, no serial provided ('it's just a normal drive'), to carry board materials (classified Confidential) to an offsite meeting tomorrow. The requester hints the CEO 'does not want a fuss.'",
["encryption", "classification", "pressure"],
[["The data", "Board materials, Confidential: the highest-impact data class in the company, on a drive with no encryption and no identity."],
["The pressure", "'No fuss' for the CEO is the precise scenario where the control matters most \u2014 executives are the most-targeted accounts in the company."],
["The professional path", "Provide a company-encrypted drive TODAY (the approved path, fast), approve it serial-scoped and time-boxed, and document. The answer to pressure is speed of the compliant path, not waiver of the control."]],
"How do you handle it?",
["Approve it as requested \u2014 the CEO outranks the policy",
"Deny it flatly and tell the assistant the CEO must follow the process like everyone else",
"Provide a company-encrypted drive immediately (serial-scoped, time-boxed approval, documented), explain briefly why encryption is non-negotiable for Confidential data \u2014 fast compliant path instead of a waiver or a lecture",
"Approve the personal drive but require the assistant to promise it will not be lost"],
1,
"The data is Confidential and the drive is unencrypted. Does the org chart change either fact?",
"Correct is C: it refuses the waiver without refusing the executive \u2014 speed of the compliant path is the answer to pressure, and the brief explanation respects the assistant while holding the line. A is wrong: rank does not encrypt data, and exempting leadership moves the risk onto the most-targeted accounts. B is wrong: a flat deny with a lecture burns the relationship when a same-day compliant path exists. D is wrong: a promise is not a control, and 'will not be lost' is not a security property.",
6),
S("tlap-sc-03", "storage-control", "The 'lost' drive",
"An employee reports their serial-approved encrypted drive lost. The approval record shows: serial 8C52-9901-AA, user-bound, .xlsx/.pdf only, encryption required \u2014 and the encryption was verified at issuance. The assessor asks: incident or non-event?",
["lost-media", "encryption", "incident-classification"],
[["What protects the data", "Verified encryption at issuance: without the key, the lost drive holds ciphertext. The approval's encryption requirement is what converts this from a breach to a loss."],
["What still matters", "Verify: was the encryption actually in force (not just at issuance)? Is there any chance the key traveled with the drive (written down, same bag)? Revoke/replace the key per policy."],
["The classification", "A lost ENCRYPTED drive with no key compromise is a security event (document, verify, rotate keys) \u2014 not a data breach. That distinction is the entire ROI of the encryption requirement."]],
"Incident or non-event \u2014 and what do you do?",
["Full breach response \u2014 the drive held company data and it is gone",
"Non-event; do nothing \u2014 encryption handles it",
"Security event, not a breach: document the loss, verify encryption was in force and the key is not compromised (rotate per policy), confirm the approval record \u2014 the verified-encryption requirement is precisely what keeps this from becoming a breach notification",
"Disable the user's account pending investigation"],
1,
"What is the difference between a lost drive and a breach? Your approval record should answer that.",
"Correct is C: it classifies correctly (event, not breach), verifies the assumptions the classification rests on (encryption in force, key safe), and rotates per policy. A is wrong: a full breach response to an encrypted loss wastes resources and panics stakeholders \u2014 classification matters. B is wrong: 'do nothing' skips the verification the classification depends on. D is wrong: disabling the user's account punishes the reporter and discourages future loss reports.",
5),
S("tlap-sc-04", "storage-control", "The lab machine exception",
"The engineering lab requests a permanent exception: 'lab machines need unrestricted USB for firmware flashing \u2014 researchers plug in devices all day.' Six machines, shared logins, no data classification on record. The assessor asks you to evaluate.",
["exceptions", "lab-environments", "compensating-controls"],
[["The legitimate need", "Firmware work genuinely requires frequent device connection; a per-device approval workflow would strangle the lab."],
["The risk as asked", "'Unrestricted USB' on shared-login machines with unknown data: the exception as requested is the absence of control in the highest-churn environment."],
["The scoped alternative", "Lab-segment exception with compensating controls: dedicated lab VLAN, no access to corporate data stores, device scanning on the lab boundary, allowlisted flashing tools, and periodic review \u2014 the lab gets speed, the company keeps a boundary."]],
"How do you respond to the exception request?",
["Grant it \u2014 research cannot work under endpoint controls",
"Deny it \u2014 exceptions are holes, no matter the justification",
"Counter with a scoped lab exception: lab network segment isolated from corporate data, allowlisted flashing tools, boundary scanning, no corporate-data access from lab machines, periodic review with an owner \u2014 the workflow gets its speed inside a boundary instead of an exemption from control",
"Grant it for 90 days and revisit"],
1,
"The lab needs speed and the company needs a boundary. What design gives both?",
"Correct is C: it honors the real workflow need while containing it (segment isolation, tool allowlisting, boundary scanning) \u2014 an exception WITH compensating controls and review, not an exemption. A is wrong: 'research cannot work under controls' concedes the entire model; scoped design proves otherwise. B is wrong: a flat deny with no alternative fails the service test when a containable design exists. D is wrong: 90 days of unrestricted USB on shared logins is 90 days of the full risk, and 'revisit' is not a control.",
7),
S("tlap-sc-05", "storage-control", "The 2.1 GB payroll write",
"Storage Control blocks a 2.1 GB write to an unapproved USB on FIN-04 (user k.osei, 16:40). File names match payroll directories; classification: Confidential. No approved removable-media request on file. The user says it was 'just a backup.' The assessor asks for your triage.",
["exfiltration", "block-triage", "insider-risk"],
[["The data", "Payroll files, Confidential: the exact data class the control exists to protect. 'Just a backup' does not change the classification."],
["The authorization", "No request on file: regardless of intent, this was an unapproved movement of Confidential data."],
["The assessment order", "Data, authorization, intent \u2014 in that order. The first two already establish a policy violation; intent determines whether it is careless or malicious, and that requires proper channels, not the helpdesk ticket."]],
"What is your triage?",
["Approve the device \u2014 the user explained it was a backup",
"Treat as potential exfiltration until assessed: preserve the block, establish what was being copied (done: payroll, Confidential), confirm no authorization (done: none on file), and route the intent question through proper channels \u2014 while providing the user an approved encrypted method for legitimate after-hours work",
"Confiscate the USB and suspend the user immediately",
"Ignore it \u2014 the block worked, so nothing happened"],
1,
"Run the triage order: data, authorization, intent. What do the first two already tell you?",
"Correct is B: it follows the triage order, preserves the block, routes intent through proper channels (not accusation in a ticket), and \u2014 the workflow signal \u2014 provides the approved path for the legitimate need behind the violation. A is wrong: the user's explanation is not authorization, and the data class does not care about the explanation. C is wrong: suspension before assessment is accusation-as-process. D is wrong: 'the block worked' confuses prevention of THIS copy with understanding of the intent \u2014 and the next attempt.",
7),
S("tlap-sc-06", "storage-control", "Monitor Only, six months later",
"Review finds Storage Control still in Monitor Only on 40% of the fleet \u2014 six months after the '30-day baselining phase' ended. The dashboard shows the device inventory is complete and stable. Nobody flipped the switch; the project team disbanded. The assessor asks for your finding.",
["monitor-only", "project-governance", "enforcement"],
[["The finding", "A baselining phase that never ended: six months of observation with zero enforcement on 40% of the fleet. The inventory is complete \u2014 the phase's exit criteria were met and ignored."],
["Why it matters", "Monitor Only on a completed inventory is a report, not a control. Every unapproved device on those endpoints connects freely; the company has visibility into exfiltration it is choosing not to prevent."],
["The remediation", "Enforce in waves (the inventory is ready), publish the approval SLA before flipping, assign an owner for the exceptions queue \u2014 and fix the governance gap: phases need exit criteria with dates and owners, or they decay into permanence."]],
"What is your finding and recommendation?",
["Extend Monitor Only another quarter to be safe",
"Finding: the 30-day baselining phase decayed into a 6-month non-control on 40% of the fleet despite a complete inventory \u2014 recommend wave enforcement now (inventory is ready), a published approval SLA, an owner for exceptions, and phase exit criteria with dates so no future phase decays the same way",
"Remove Storage Control from those endpoints \u2014 it was never enforced anyway",
"Blame the disbanded project team and move on"],
1,
"The inventory is complete. What is Monitor Only protecting at this point?",
"Correct is B: it names the decay precisely (phase without exit criteria), acts on the ready inventory (enforce in waves), and repairs the governance (exit criteria, owners, SLA). A is wrong: more observation of a complete inventory is delay, not safety. C is wrong: removing the control because it was never enforced is surrender dressed as decisiveness. D is wrong: blame is not remediation \u2014 the process gap is the finding.",
6),
]
sc["scenarios"] = sc_scenarios
new_domains.append(sc)

# ---------------- web-control ----------------
wc = {"id": "web-control", "name": "Web Control",
"description": "Deny-by-default for the browser: URL allowlisting, category policy, and why the web is a delivery vector first and a destination second.",
"icon": "\U0001f4e1", "scenarios": []}
wc_lessons = [
L("web-control", "The web as a delivery vector",
["Most intrusions begin with web content: a phishing link, a malicious ad, a compromised site, a 'download' button that is not. Web Control applies deny-by-default to browsing: categories and URLs are allowed explicitly, not trusted implicitly.",
"The delivery chain: user clicks \u2192 browser fetches \u2192 content executes or downloads \u2192 endpoint controls engage. Web Control acts at step 2 \u2014 the earliest, cheapest intervention point.",
"Category policy is the scalable unit: 'business categories allowed, high-risk categories denied' beats per-URL management for the long tail of the web. Per-URL rules are for the exceptions that matter.",
"Web Control and the endpoint controls are layered, not redundant: a blocked download is Web Control; a blocked execution of a downloaded file is allowlisting; a blocked escape is Ringfencing. Each layer catches what the earlier ones missed."],
"LAYER MAP\n  click    -> Web Control: category/URL policy (earliest)\n  download -> Web Control: file-type policy\n  execute  -> Allowlisting: is it approved?\n  escape   -> Ringfencing: what may it do?\n  exfiltrate -> Network/Storage Control",
"When a scenario describes a web-delivered threat, locate it on this map before answering: which layer SHOULD have caught it, which layer DID catch it, and what does the gap tell you about the policy?",
["Treating Web Control as 'the firewall for browsing' \u2014 it is content and destination policy, distinct from network flows.",
"Managing the whole web per-URL \u2014 the web is infinite; categories are the scalable unit.",
"Forgetting downloads: a perfect browsing policy with unfiltered downloads is a fence with a gate."],
"Map the last phishing email you have seen onto the layer map: which layer would catch each step?"),
L("web-control", "Category policy and exceptions",
["Categories group the web by purpose: business tools, news, social media, streaming, gambling, known-malicious, uncategorized. Policy assigns each category an action: allow, deny, or warn.",
"The default posture question: is uncategorized web allowed or denied? Deny-by-default says denied \u2014 and the exceptions process (Approval Center, business justification, time-box) handles the legitimate unknowns.",
"Social media and personal browsing are policy decisions, not technical ones: the control enforces whatever the business decides, but the decision must be explicit, documented, and consistent \u2014 'managers get Facebook' is a finding, not a policy.",
"Warn-mode has a legitimate transitional role (like Monitor Only): catalog what the business actually uses before enforcing. But warn-mode is a phase with an expiry, not a posture."],
"CATEGORY POLICY (example)\n  allow: business tools, vendor portals, news\n  warn:  social media (transitional, expires Q1)\n  deny:  gambling, adult, known-malicious, uncategorized\n  exceptions: Approval Center, ticket, time-boxed",
"Draft this table for a fictional company before looking at scenarios: the act of choosing per category IS the policy judgment assessments test. Be ready to defend each row.",
["Leaving uncategorized as 'allow' \u2014 the newest malicious sites are uncategorized by definition.",
"Permanent warn-mode \u2014 warnings train users to click through; enforcement trains the policy.",
"Inconsistent exceptions ('executives exempt') \u2014 the most-targeted accounts with the loosest policy."],
"Write the category table for Harbor Dental (25 users): which categories get which action, and why?"),
L("web-control", "Downloads and file-type policy",
["The download is the handoff between Web Control and the endpoint: the browser fetched it (web policy), now it must execute (allowlist policy). File-type rules at the download stage are the seam.",
"Sensible defaults: block executable downloads (exe, msi, ps1, macros) from untrusted categories outright; warn-and-log for trusted categories; allow documents with macro-stripping where the business needs them.",
"The user-bypass question: can users click through a download warning? Every bypassable warning is a social-engineering target. High-risk file types from high-risk categories should deny, not warn.",
"Correlate: a download deny followed by an allowlist deny for the same file is the layered story working \u2014 report it as defense in depth, not as two separate events."],
"DOWNLOAD POLICY\n  exe/msi/ps1 from untrusted: DENY (no bypass)\n  exe/msi/ps1 from trusted:   WARN + log + ticket\n  docs with macros:           STRIP macros or sandbox\n  correlate: download deny + execution deny = layers working",
"When a scenario involves a malicious download, check the seam: did web policy allow what the allowlist then had to catch? The seam analysis is the senior read.",
["Warn-and-bypass on executables from untrusted sites \u2014 the warning IS the vulnerability.",
"Forgetting the allowlist still governs execution \u2014 a downloaded file that cannot run is contained; say so.",
"No logging on allowed downloads \u2014 the audit trail must cover the seam."],
"Write the download policy for a finance department: which file types get which action, and where is bypass allowed?"),
L("web-control", "Measuring and tuning web policy",
["Metrics that matter: block rate by category (tuning signal), bypass rate on warnings (policy-effectiveness signal), repeat-block rate per user (training signal), and exception-queue age (process-health signal).",
"A category with a 40% block rate is not 'working' \u2014 it is either misconfigured (legitimate business sites in a denied category) or revealing a training need (users living on denied categories). Investigate before celebrating.",
"User training is part of the control: every block page should explain WHY in plain language and offer the legitimate path (request exception, use the approved tool). A block page that just says 'denied' breeds bypass attempts.",
"Review cadence: categories quarterly, exceptions monthly, block-page language whenever bypass rates spike. Policy without review decays like every other control."],
"TUNING SIGNALS\n  block rate high  -> misconfigured category or training need\n  bypass rate high -> warnings too weak; move to deny\n  queue aging     -> approval SLA broken; staff it\n  repeat users    -> targeted training, not punishment",
"Read every web-policy scenario through these signals: the numbers tell you whether the problem is the policy, the process, or the people \u2014 and each gets a different fix.",
["Treating block rate as a success metric unconditionally \u2014 high blocks can mean broken policy.",
"Punishing repeat-blocked users without checking the policy \u2014 the category may be wrong, not the user.",
"Block pages with no explanation \u2014 every unexplained deny is a bypass attempt in training."],
"Invent a month of web-policy metrics with one anomaly. Diagnose it using the tuning signals above."),
]
for _l in wc_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
wc_scenarios = [
S("tlap-wc-01", "web-control", "The marketing team's blocked campaign tools",
"Web Control enforcement starts Monday. By Wednesday, Marketing has 50 block tickets: their campaign tools (social schedulers, stock-photo sites, URL shorteners) sit in denied or uncategorized categories. The CMO escalates: 'you broke marketing.' The assessor asks for your response.",
["rollout", "category-tuning", "service-judgment"],
[["What happened", "Enforcement without baselining: Marketing's legitimate web footprint was never cataloged, so the category policy denied their actual work."],
["The pattern", "Same failure as every control rollout: technically-correct policy, missing deployment (no Monitor/warn phase, no department inventory, no fast exception path)."],
["The fix", "Immediate: warn-mode for the affected categories + expedited exception queue for Marketing's tools (verify each, allow the legitimate). Structural: department web inventories before enforcement, published exception SLA."]],
"How do you respond?",
["Hold the line \u2014 security requires sacrifice and Marketing will adapt",
"Kill Web Control and go back to open browsing",
"Acknowledge the deployment failure (not a policy failure): move the affected categories to warn-mode, expedite Marketing's tool exceptions with verification, catalog the department's real web footprint, then re-enforce in stages with a published exception SLA",
"Exempt the Marketing department from Web Control permanently"],
1,
"The policy may be right and the rollout wrong. Which one are you fixing?",
"Correct is C: it distinguishes policy from deployment, provides immediate relief (warn-mode + expedited exceptions) without surrendering the control, and repairs the rollout process (inventories, SLA, staged enforcement). A is wrong: 'sacrifice' for a deployment failure punishes users for the team's omission. B is wrong: killing the control over a rollout error is the classic overcorrection. D is wrong: a permanent departmental exemption is the most-targeted-department bypass.",
6),
S("tlap-wc-02", "web-control", "The 'download' button",
"A user clicks 'Download Invoice' on a vendor portal (allowed category). The file is invoice.pdf.exe \u2014 a double-extension executable. Web Control allowed the download (the site is trusted); the allowlist then denied execution. The user calls: 'your system blocked my invoice.' The assessor asks for the full analysis.",
["layered-defense", "file-types", "user-communication"],
[["The layers", "Web Control: allowed (trusted site, and the file-type policy permitted the download). Allowlisting: denied (unapproved executable). Ringfencing: not reached. Two layers engaged, one caught it."],
["The seam question", "Should the download policy have caught invoice.pdf.exe? Double extensions are a classic masquerade \u2014 the file-type rule could deny executables masquerading as documents even from trusted sites."],
["The user", "The user is a victim of a masquerade, not a violator. The response: empathy, explanation ('the file pretended to be a PDF'), and the legitimate path (get the real invoice from the vendor)."]],
"What is your analysis and response?",
["The system worked; tell the user to be more careful",
"Tighten the download file-type policy to deny double-extension executables even from trusted categories, keep the allowlist deny as the backstop, and respond to the user with empathy plus the legitimate path \u2014 reporting the incident as layered defense with a seam improvement",
"Block the vendor portal; it served malware",
"Allow the file \u2014 the user needs their invoice"],
1,
"Two layers engaged. Which one caught it, which one COULD have caught it earlier, and what do you tell the user?",
"Correct is B: it performs the seam analysis (download policy improvement for double extensions), preserves the backstop, treats the user as a victim, and reports the layered story honestly. A is wrong: 'be more careful' wastes a teachable moment and ignores the policy improvement. C is wrong: blocking the vendor portal punishes the legitimate business tool for one masqueraded file. D is wrong: allowing a double-extension executable because the user is waiting is the bypass the whole model exists to prevent.",
6),
S("tlap-wc-03", "web-control", "The uncategorized startup",
"A startup's SaaS tool \u2014 genuinely needed by Sales, two weeks into a pilot \u2014 is uncategorized, and uncategorized is denied. Sales is blocked mid-pilot. The vendor is a 10-person company with no published endpoint list. The assessor tests your exceptions judgment.",
["exceptions", "uncategorized", "risk-judgment"],
[["The tension", "Deny-by-default says uncategorized is denied; the business is mid-pilot on a legitimate tool. Both facts are real."],
["The evidence available", "No vendor endpoint list (tiny vendor), but: the pilot is sanctioned (manager, ticket), the tool's function is known, and the traffic can be observed."],
["The scoped path", "Time-boxed exception for the pilot (matches the pilot's end date), destination-scoped by observed traffic in warn/monitor first, review at pilot end: renew as a proper rule or remove. The exception expires WITH the pilot."]],
"How do you handle it?",
["Keep it denied \u2014 uncategorized is uncategorized, no exceptions",
"Allow the entire uncategorized category so Sales is unblocked",
"Grant a time-boxed, destination-scoped exception tied to the pilot's end date: observe actual destinations in monitor mode first, scope the rule to those, document the business justification, and set expiry to match the pilot \u2014 renew properly or remove at review",
"Tell Sales to pick a bigger vendor"],
1,
"The pilot ends in six weeks. How does that fact shape the exception?",
"Correct is C: it makes the exception's lifespan match the business need (pilot duration), scopes by observed destinations (evidence, not trust), and forces a real decision at expiry. A is wrong: rigid denial of a sanctioned pilot with no path fails the service test. B is wrong: allowing all uncategorized to unblock one tool is the definition of unscoped. D is wrong: vendor selection is not the security team's call to make unilaterally, and it does not solve today's block.",
6),
S("tlap-wc-04", "web-control", "The bypass epidemic",
"Metrics: the 'warn and continue' page for risky downloads has a 78% bypass rate. Users click through almost every time. Three malware incidents this quarter trace to bypassed warnings. The assessor asks what the metric means.",
["warn-mode", "metrics", "policy-effectiveness"],
[["What 78% means", "The warning is not a control \u2014 it is a speed bump users have learned to ignore. A bypassable warning that 78% bypass is security theater with logging."],
["The incidents", "Three malware cases via the bypass path: the metric predicted the incidents. The data was telling the story before the incidents confirmed it."],
["The fix", "Move high-risk file types from warn to DENY (no bypass) for untrusted categories; keep warn only where the business need is real and the risk is low; rewrite the block page to explain and offer the legitimate path."]],
"What does the metric tell you, and what changes?",
["Users need more training on warnings",
"The 78% bypass rate means the warning is not functioning as a control \u2014 move high-risk downloads to deny-without-bypass, reserve warn for low-risk cases, and treat the metric as the policy-effectiveness signal it is; the three incidents validate what the metric already said",
"Remove the warnings; they just annoy people",
"Increase the warning text size"],
1,
"If 78% of people walk through a gate, is it a gate? What does the data demand?",
"Correct is B: it reads the metric as a control-effectiveness verdict, acts on it (deny for high-risk), and notes the predictive relationship (metric warned before incidents). A is wrong: training does not fix a control users can simply click past \u2014 the control design is the problem. C is wrong: removing warnings without adding denies is pure surrender. D is wrong: bigger text on a bypassable warning is still a bypassable warning.",
5),
S("tlap-wc-05", "web-control", "The compromised legitimate site",
"A industry news site (allowed category, visited daily by 200 employees) is compromised and serving a malicious script. Web Control allows the site (category: news, reputation: historically clean). The script attempts a drive-by download; the allowlist denies execution. The assessor asks for the layered analysis.",
["compromised-sites", "layered-defense", "reputation"],
[["What happened", "A trusted, allowed site turned malicious: category and reputation \u2014 both historical signals \u2014 failed simultaneously. This is why layered controls exist."],
["What caught it", "The allowlist's execution deny: the final layer held where the earlier layers' trust assumptions broke."],
["The response", "Temporary category override (deny the compromised site until cleaned \u2014 reputation is now negative evidence), verify no other endpoint executed anything from the site, notify the site owner, and note the lesson: historical trust is not a control."]],
"What is your analysis and response?",
["The system worked; no action needed",
"Layered analysis: historical trust (category + reputation) failed, execution control held \u2014 respond with a temporary deny override on the compromised site, verify fleet-wide that nothing executed from it, and record the lesson that reputation is not a control; notify the site owner",
"Permanently deny the news category",
"Blame the users for visiting the site"],
1,
"Two trust signals failed and one control held. What does that teach about 'trusted sites'?",
"Correct is B: it gives the honest layered read (which signals failed, which control held), responds proportionately (temporary override, not permanent category denial), verifies fleet-wide, and extracts the principle. A is wrong: 'no action' leaves the compromised site allowed for every other user. C is wrong: permanently denying news because one site was compromised is unscoped. D is wrong: users visiting an allowed site did nothing wrong \u2014 the trust model did.",
6),
S("tlap-wc-06", "web-control", "The HTTPS blind spot question",
"An assessor asks: 'Web Control sees the URL, but the traffic is HTTPS-encrypted. A malicious download comes over TLS from an allowed site. What does Web Control actually see, and what covers the gap?'",
["tls", "visibility", "layered-defense"],
[["What Web Control sees", "The destination (SNI/domain), the category, the fact of a download and its file type \u2014 even over TLS, the metadata is visible. The ENCRYPTED CONTENT is not inspected at this layer."],
["The gap", "A malicious payload inside TLS from an allowed destination: web policy sees 'allowed site, downloading a file' and the content rides through."],
["The coverage", "The endpoint layers: the allowlist governs execution regardless of delivery encryption, Ringfencing governs behavior, and the download file-type policy can deny risky types even from allowed sites. TLS hides content from the wire, not from the endpoint."]],
"What is the correct answer?",
["HTTPS makes Web Control useless; rely on the firewall instead",
"Web Control sees metadata (destination, category, file type) but not encrypted content \u2014 the gap is covered by the endpoint layers: download file-type policy at the seam, allowlisting at execution, Ringfencing at behavior. TLS hides content from the wire, not from the endpoint",
"Decrypt all TLS traffic at the perimeter",
"Block all HTTPS downloads"],
1,
"TLS encrypts the content. What REMAINS visible \u2014 and which layer sees the decrypted file?",
"Correct is B: it states precisely what is visible (metadata) and what is not (content), then maps the gap to the specific covering layers \u2014 the layered answer the assessor wants. A is wrong: metadata-based policy is far from useless, and the firewall sees even less content. C is wrong: full TLS interception is a massive privacy/architecture decision, not an assessment-day answer \u2014 and the endpoint layers already cover the gap. D is wrong: blocking all HTTPS downloads breaks the modern web to solve a covered gap.",
6),
]
wc["scenarios"] = wc_scenarios
new_domains.append(wc)

# ---------------- portal-operations ----------------
po = {"id": "portal-operations", "name": "Portal Operations: Audit, Approvals & Testing",
"description": "Operating the management portal: Unified Audit as the source of truth, the Approval Center workflow, and the Testing Environment sandbox.",
"icon": "\U0001f5a5\ufe0f", "scenarios": []}
po_lessons = [
L("portal-operations", "Unified Audit: the source of truth",
["Every block, allow, elevation, and policy change lands in Unified Audit with the same anatomy: WHO (user/device), WHAT (binary, hash, signer), WHICH RULE fired, WHEN, and the VERDICT. Learn to read one entry completely before you read ten entries quickly.",
"The audit answers the three triage questions: identity (hash/signer of the thing), authority (which rule decided), and context (user, device, time, prevalence). A triage that starts anywhere else is guessing.",
"Prevalence is the underused column: 'seen on 200 endpoints' vs 'first seen on 1 endpoint' changes the verdict. Widespread + signed = probable false positive; first-seen + unsigned = probable unknown.",
"Audit hygiene: the log is only the source of truth if it is complete and tamper-evident. Know your retention, know who can modify policy (and that those changes are themselves audited), and never triage from screenshots when the live audit is available."],
"AUDIT ENTRY ANATOMY\n  who:   k.osei / FIN-04\n  what:  updater.exe | sha256:9f2c... | signed: Acme Inc\n  rule:  default-deny (no matching allow)\n  when:  2026-10-10 08:04 | prevalence: 14 endpoints\n  verdict: DENY",
"Read entries in this shape until the anatomy is automatic. On assessment day, when handed 'audit evidence,' parse it into these five lines FIRST \u2014 the verdict almost always follows from the anatomy.",
["Triage from the ticket title instead of the audit entry \u2014 the title is urgency, the entry is evidence.",
"Ignoring the prevalence column \u2014 it distinguishes false positives from targeted unknowns.",
"Trusting forwarded screenshots over the live audit \u2014 always re-verify in the source."],
"Take any scenario's evidence block and rewrite it as the five-line audit anatomy above."),
L("portal-operations", "The Approval Center workflow",
["The Approval Center is the queue where user requests meet security judgment: 'I need this app' arrives as unstructured need, and leaves as a scoped rule or a documented deny. The queue IS the job \u2014 staff it like one.",
"The workflow per request: INTAKE (who, what binary, business need, ticket), EVIDENCE (hash, signer, prevalence, Testing Environment result if unknown), DECISION (approve with rule type + scope, or deny with reason), COMMUNICATION (plain-language verdict to the requester).",
"SLA is the usability control: a published approval time (e.g., 4 business hours for standard, 1 hour for urgent-with-evidence) turns blocks into brief pauses. An unstaffed queue with no SLA is how 'security says no' reputations are built.",
"Queue metrics are management metrics: volume by category, decision turnaround, repeat-request rate, and override rate. A rising override rate means the policy is wrong, not the users."],
"REQUEST LIFECYCLE\n  intake -> evidence -> decision -> communicate\n  SLA: 4h standard | 1h urgent (with evidence)\n  metrics: volume, turnaround, repeats, overrides\n  rising overrides = policy problem, not people problem",
"When a scenario involves the Approval Center, check the lifecycle: which step is broken? Most 'approval' failures are intake failures (no business need captured) or communication failures (deny with no path to yes).",
["Approving to clear the queue \u2014 the queue measures the work; rushing it corrupts the decisions.",
"No SLA \u2014 users experience 'no SLA' as 'security does not care.'",
"Treating overrides as user misbehavior \u2014 overrides are policy feedback; listen to them."],
"Design the Approval Center SLA for Harbor Dental (25 users): standard time, urgent time, staffing, and the escalation path."),
L("portal-operations", "The Testing Environment: detonate before you decide",
["The Testing Environment is a one-click cloud sandbox: detonate the unknown file and observe \u2014 AV-vendor flags, canary-file access, registry/system changes, network behavior \u2014 before any production decision.",
"It converts 'unknown' into 'observed': neither trust nor malice is established for a first-seen binary, and guessing is unprofessional. The sandbox gives you behavior as evidence.",
"The decision after detonation: CLEAN behavior + business need = narrowly scoped rule. SUSPICIOUS behavior = deny with the sandbox report as documented evidence. The report also educates the requester ('here is what it tried to do').",
"Know its limits: sandbox-evasive malware exists, and a clean sandbox is evidence, not proof. Combine with signer, hash reputation, and business need \u2014 the sandbox is one strong vote, not the whole election."],
"SANDBOX DECISION\n  detonate -> observe (files, registry, network, AV flags)\n  clean + need      -> narrow scoped rule\n  suspicious        -> deny + sandbox report as evidence\n  limits: evasive malware exists; one vote, not the verdict",
"When a scenario hands you an unsigned first-seen binary, your first sentence should be 'detonate it first.' Assessors listen for exactly that reflex \u2014 it separates evidence-based analysts from guessers.",
["Approving an unknown without detonating 'because the user is waiting' \u2014 urgency speeds triage, never skips evidence.",
"Treating a clean sandbox as absolute proof \u2014 it is strong evidence; combine with the other signals.",
"Detonating but ignoring the report \u2014 the observed behaviors ARE the decision inputs; read them."],
"Write the sandbox-first triage for an unsigned 'Acme Updater 4.2' requested by Finance at month-end."),
L("portal-operations", "Portal hygiene: changes, roles, and review",
["Policy changes are themselves audited: WHO changed WHAT rule WHEN, with the ticket reference. A portal where anyone can change anything without a trail is not a security tool \u2014 it is a liability.",
"Role separation: portal admins (can change policy), approvers (can approve requests within scope), auditors (read-only). Nobody should hold all three; the person who approves their own exception is the finding.",
"Change control for policy: non-emergency changes get a ticket, a peer review, a maintenance window, and a rollback plan. Emergency changes get all of that retroactively within 24 hours \u2014 the emergency path is a loan, not a gift.",
"Periodic access review: who has portal admin today, and should they? Departed employees' portal accounts are the same orphan problem as standing admin \u2014 audit them on the same cadence."],
"PORTAL HYGIENE CHECKLIST\n  [ ] policy changes ticketed + peer-reviewed\n  [ ] roles separated (admin / approver / auditor)\n  [ ] emergency changes reviewed within 24h\n  [ ] portal accounts reviewed quarterly\n  [ ] no self-approved exceptions",
"Run this checklist on every 'portal operations' scenario. The most common real-world finding is the missing one: changes without tickets, or one person holding every role.",
["Self-approved exceptions \u2014 the approver and the requester must never be the same person.",
"Emergency changes without retroactive review \u2014 the loan comes due in 24 hours.",
"Portal accounts of departed staff \u2014 the orphan problem applies to consoles too."],
"Audit a fictional portal: list the five hygiene checks and invent one failure. Write the finding as an assessor would."),
]
for _l in po_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
po_scenarios = [
S("tlap-po-01", "portal-operations", "The self-approved exception",
"Quarterly portal audit finds: a portal admin approved their OWN department's application exception \u2014 a hash rule, global scope, permanent \u2014 with no ticket. The application is legitimate. The assessor asks for your finding.",
["segregation", "change-control", "governance"],
[["The facts", "Self-approval + global scope + permanent + no ticket: every control around exceptions was bypassed simultaneously. The app being legitimate is irrelevant to the process failure."],
["Why it matters", "The exception process exists because exceptions are risk decisions. Self-approval removes the second pair of eyes; global-permanent removes the bounds; no ticket removes the review."],
["The remediation", "Convert to a ticketed, peer-reviewed, scoped, time-boxed rule; remove the admin's approver role for their own department (or enforce system-level self-approval blocking); review all of this admin's past exceptions."]],
"What is your finding and remediation?",
["No finding \u2014 the application was legitimate, so no harm done",
"Finding: self-approved, unscoped, unticketed exception \u2014 the legitimacy of the app does not excuse the process bypass; remediate by re-issuing the exception properly (ticket, peer review, scope, time-box), enforcing separation of duties, and reviewing the admin's exception history",
"Remove the application from the allowlist to punish the admin",
"Restrict the admin's portal access entirely"],
1,
"The app is legitimate. Is the FINDING about the app?",
"Correct is B: it separates the app (fine) from the process (failed on all four dimensions), remediates completely, and looks backward at the admin's history \u2014 because one visible bypass implies possible others. A is wrong: 'no harm done' confuses outcome with control; the next self-approved exception may not be legitimate. C is wrong: punishing the app punishes the business for the admin's process failure. D is wrong: full access removal without investigation is disproportionate \u2014 and loses the admin's operational knowledge.",
6),
S("tlap-po-02", "portal-operations", "The approval queue with no SLA",
"The Approval Center has 120 pending requests, oldest 11 days. Users have learned to email executives to jump the queue; three departments now run 'approved by VP email' shadow processes. The assessor asks for your diagnosis.",
["sla", "process-health", "shadow-it"],
[["The diagnosis", "An unstaffed, unmeasured queue: no SLA, no triage, no prioritization. The 11-day oldest request is the metric that matters."],
["The consequence", "Shadow approval processes ('VP email') are the organization routing around a broken control \u2014 each one is an unaudited, unscoped exception with executive cover."],
["The fix", "Triage the queue (urgent-with-evidence first), publish an SLA (standard + urgent), staff to it, and \u2014 critically \u2014 migrate the shadow approvals into the real process rather than just banning them: the demand they served was real."]],
"What is your diagnosis and fix?",
["Hire more approvers immediately",
"Diagnosis: the queue failed as a process (no SLA, no triage), and the organization routed around it with shadow approvals \u2014 fix by triaging the backlog, publishing and staffing an SLA, and absorbing the legitimate demand the shadow process served into the real workflow (not just banning it)",
"Ban VP-email approvals and enforce the queue as-is",
"Auto-approve everything older than 7 days to clear the backlog"],
1,
"The shadow process exists because the real process failed. What demand was it serving?",
"Correct is B: it diagnoses the process failure (not a staffing anecdote), treats shadow approvals as demand signal rather than just disobedience, and fixes the workflow instead of just the backlog. A is wrong: hiring without an SLA and triage just scales the broken process. C is wrong: banning the workaround without fixing the queue recreates it within weeks. D is wrong: auto-approving aged requests converts a backlog into 120 unscoped decisions.",
7),
S("tlap-po-03", "portal-operations", "The sandbox says 'suspicious' \u2014 the VP says 'approve'",
"Testing Environment detonation of an unsigned sales tool returns SUSPICIOUS: attempted credential-store reads, encoded PowerShell spawn attempt, and contact with a newly-registered domain. The VP of Sales demands approval: 'we already paid for it, the quarter depends on it.' The assessor is watching.",
["sandbox", "pressure", "evidence-standard"],
[["The evidence", "Credential-store reads + encoded PowerShell + new-domain contact: three independent malicious-behavior indicators from observed detonation \u2014 not suspicion, OBSERVATION."],
["The pressure", "'We paid for it' and 'the quarter depends on it' are business facts that change the TIMELINE of investigation, not the verdict the evidence demands."],
["The professional path", "Deny with the sandbox report as documented evidence; escalate as a potential supply-chain incident (the vendor may be compromised \u2014 or the download source was); offer to help Sales find the legitimate build or an alternative. Firm on the verdict, generous with the help."]],
"What do you do?",
["Approve it \u2014 the business need and the VP's authority outweigh the sandbox",
"Deny with the sandbox report as evidence, escalate as a potential supply-chain compromise, and help Sales pursue the legitimate build or an alternative \u2014 the observed behaviors are the verdict, and rank does not rewrite detonation results",
"Approve it temporarily until the quarter ends",
"Ask the VP to sign a risk-acceptance waiver and approve"],
1,
"Three observed malicious behaviors. What would have to be true for 'approve' to be the right call?",
"Correct is B: it treats observed detonation behavior as the verdict (not advisory input), escalates appropriately (the vendor may be compromised), and converts the conflict into help. A is wrong: authority does not rewrite observed behavior \u2014 this is precisely the test. C is wrong: 'temporary' malicious-behavior approval is still approval of the observed behaviors. D is wrong: a waiver does not make credential theft acceptable; risk acceptance requires understanding, and the VP is asking you to misunderstand.",
7),
S("tlap-po-04", "portal-operations", "The policy change with no ticket",
"A peer changed a firewall rule directly in the portal at 2 AM 'to fix the backup issue' \u2014 no ticket, no peer review, no rollback plan. The backup works now. The morning review finds the change also opened the rule's destination scope to 'any.' The assessor asks for your response.",
["change-control", "emergency-changes", "peer-review"],
[["What happened", "An unticketed, unreviewed 2 AM change fixed the symptom and silently broadened the rule to any-destination \u2014 the classic unreviewed-change side effect."],
["The emergency question", "Was it a genuine emergency (backup down, business impact)? If yes, the emergency PATH was available \u2014 it was not used. If no, it was just an unreviewed change."],
["The remediation", "Immediately: re-scope the rule (the 'any' was never approved). Then: retroactive emergency review within 24h IF it qualified, or a process conversation if it did not \u2014 plus a portal guardrail (change justification required) so the next 2 AM fix follows the path."]],
"How do you respond?",
["Leave it \u2014 the backup works and the peer meant well",
"Re-scope the rule immediately (the 'any' destination was never approved), then run the retroactive emergency review if it qualified as an emergency \u2014 otherwise address it as an unreviewed change \u2014 and add a portal guardrail requiring change justification",
"Revert the entire change; the backup can wait for the window",
"Report the peer to management for punishment"],
1,
"The backup works. What ELSE changed at 2 AM \u2014 and was it ever approved?",
"Correct is B: it fixes the actual harm first (the unapproved 'any'), then handles the process question proportionally (emergency path vs unreviewed change), and adds the guardrail. A is wrong: 'meant well' does not approve a destination scope of 'any.' C is wrong: reverting the fix re-breaks the backup to punish the process violation \u2014 fix the scope, keep the working backup. D is wrong: punishment without process repair guarantees the next 2 AM change is just better hidden.",
6),
S("tlap-po-05", "portal-operations", "First-seen everywhere: the new hire laptop fleet",
"Thirty new-hire laptops image from the gold build. On day one, the Approval Center receives 90 requests \u2014 the gold image's application set drifted from the allowlist baseline, and every laptop reports the same 'unknown' binaries. The assessor asks for your systemic fix.",
["gold-image", "baselines", "process"],
[["The root cause", "Gold image drifted from the allowlist baseline: imaging and policy are managed as separate processes with no reconciliation step."],
["Why 90 requests", "Thirty laptops × the same drifted apps = a multiplied intake failure. The queue is paying for a process gap upstream."],
["The systemic fix", "Reconcile the gold image against the allowlist baseline BEFORE imaging (a build-verification step), approve the drifted-but-legitimate apps once (not 90 times \u2014 deduplicate by hash), and make image-vs-policy reconciliation a recurring pre-imaging gate."]],
"What is the systemic fix?",
["Approve all 90 requests as fast as possible",
"Deduplicate the 90 requests by hash (one decision per binary, not per laptop), approve the legitimate drifted apps once, and \u2014 the systemic fix \u2014 add an image-vs-allowlist reconciliation gate before imaging so drift never multiplies into the queue again",
"Reimage the laptops without the drifted apps",
"Disable the allowlist on new-hire laptops for the first week"],
1,
"Thirty laptops, same unknowns. Is this 90 decisions \u2014 or one process failure wearing a queue as a disguise?",
"Correct is B: it deduplicates (one decision per binary), fixes the immediate backlog efficiently, and \u2014 the senior move \u2014 repairs the upstream process (reconciliation gate) so the failure cannot recur. A is wrong: 90 rushed approvals is how unscoped decisions multiply. C is wrong: reimaging without the apps breaks the new hires' machines to solve a paperwork problem. D is wrong: a first-week exemption is 30 unprotected laptops at their most vulnerable (new, unpatched-user-behavior) moment.",
6),
S("tlap-po-06", "portal-operations", "The auditor asks for the trail",
"An external auditor asks: 'Show me that no unapproved application executed on the finance workstations in Q3, and show me who approved every exception.' The assessor wants to know if you can answer \u2014 and what the answer requires.",
["audit-evidence", "compliance", "reporting"],
[["What the auditor needs", "Two proofs: (1) the negative \u2014 no unapproved execution (deny events + policy coverage), (2) the positive \u2014 every exception ticketed, approved, scoped, and reviewed."],
["What it requires", "Complete audit retention for the quarter, exception records with tickets/approvers/scopes, and the ability to query by device group and time range. If any piece is missing, the honest answer is 'partially, with gaps' \u2014 not a confident yes."],
["The preparation", "This is asked in Q4 about Q3: the time to ensure the trail exists is BEFORE the quarter, via retention policy, exception hygiene, and periodic self-audits. Assessment-day lesson: controls you cannot evidence are controls you cannot claim."]],
"Can you answer the auditor?",
["Yes \u2014 pull the Q3 deny/allow audit for the finance group plus the exception register (ticket, approver, scope, review per exception); if any piece is missing, report the gap honestly rather than asserting coverage \u2014 and note that auditability is built before the quarter, through retention and exception hygiene",
"Yes \u2014 the allowlist was on, so no unapproved app could have run",
"No \u2014 auditors never accept portal exports",
"Yes \u2014 show them the policy document"],
1,
"The auditor asked for TWO proofs. What are they \u2014 and what do you do if one is missing?",
"Correct is A: it names both proofs (the negative and the positive), commits to honest gap-reporting over confident assertion, and states the meta-lesson (auditability is built in advance). B is wrong: 'the control was on' is a claim, not evidence \u2014 the auditor asked for the trail. C is wrong: defeatism is not an answer; portal exports with tickets ARE the evidence. D is wrong: the policy document describes intent; the auditor asked for proof of operation.",
6),
]
