# ---------------- deny-by-default-reasoning ----------------
dr = {"id": "deny-by-default-reasoning", "name": "Deny-by-Default Reasoning Under Pressure",
"description": "The judgment capstone: applying default-deny thinking to ambiguous, urgent, and adversarial situations \u2014 the scenarios assessors use to separate operators from memorizers.",
"icon": "\U0001f9e0", "scenarios": []}
dr_lessons = [
L("deny-by-default-reasoning", "The inversion: thinking in allowlists",
["Default-allow thinking asks 'is there a reason to block this?' \u2014 and unknown things pass for lack of a reason. Default-deny thinking asks 'is there a reason to ALLOW this?' \u2014 and unknown things wait for evidence. The inversion is a habit, not a slogan.",
"Practice the inversion on every request: the requester says 'I need this app.' The default-deny analyst hears 'here is a claim of need; where is the evidence of trust?' Need is the START of the workflow, not the END of the decision.",
"The inversion applies beyond execution: network destinations ('why should this app reach there?'), elevation ('why should this task have admin?'), storage ('why should this data leave?'). One mental motion, every control.",
"Under pressure, the inversion is your anchor: urgency argues 'allow first, check later.' The inversion answers 'triage faster, same standard.' Speed up the evidence gathering \u2014 never skip it."],
"THE INVERSION\n  allow-thinking: 'any reason to block?' -> unknown passes\n  deny-thinking:   'any reason to ALLOW?' -> unknown waits\n  pressure says:   'allow first, check later'\n  answer:          'triage FASTER, same standard'",
"Run the inversion on your next five practice scenarios before reading the choices: write the 'reason to allow' the evidence actually supports. If you cannot write one, the answer is not 'approve.'",
["Inverting only execution \u2014 apply it to network, elevation, and storage too.",
"Confusing 'evidence of need' with 'evidence of trust' \u2014 the ticket proves need; the signer proves trust.",
"Letting urgency flip the question \u2014 pressure changes timelines, never the question."],
"Take three past scenarios and rewrite each as the 'reason to allow' the evidence supports \u2014 or state that none exists."),
L("deny-by-default-reasoning", "Ambiguity: deciding with incomplete evidence",
["Real triage is decided with incomplete evidence: the signer is unfamiliar, the sandbox is still running, the user is waiting. The professional skill is deciding WELL under uncertainty \u2014 not waiting for certainty that never comes.",
"The method: list what you KNOW (evidence), list what you ASSUME (and mark it), choose the LEAST-PRIVILEGE action consistent with both, and set the REVIEW trigger ('revisit when the sandbox completes'). Decided, bounded, revisable.",
"State assumptions explicitly: 'assuming the signer verifies, a scoped certificate rule is appropriate; if it does not verify, this becomes a deny.' Assessors award credit for stated assumptions \u2014 they are the mark of a thinking analyst.",
"Least privilege under uncertainty means: narrower scope, shorter time-box, more logging \u2014 not paralysis. 'I cannot decide' is rarely the right answer; 'I decide narrowly and revisit' usually is."],
"AMBIGUITY PROTOCOL\n  know:   signed, unknown publisher, hash clean reputation\n  assume: publisher is legitimate (UNVERIFIED)\n  act:    narrow hash rule, 7-day time-box, enhanced logging\n  review: when signer verification completes",
"Practice the protocol on ambiguous scenarios: the four lines force you to separate knowledge from assumption \u2014 and the time-box plus review trigger make the decision safe to make.",
["Deciding as if assumptions were facts \u2014 mark every assumption explicitly.",
"Paralysis disguised as thoroughness \u2014 narrow-and-revisable beats frozen.",
"Forgetting the review trigger \u2014 a provisional decision without a revisit is just a decision."],
"Take an ambiguous scenario and write the four-line protocol: know, assume, act, review."),
L("deny-by-default-reasoning", "Adversarial thinking: how would I abuse this?",
["For every rule you write or review, ask the attacker's question: 'how would I abuse this grant?' The rule that survives adversarial review is the rule that survives attackers.",
"The abuse catalog: path rules \u2192 drop a file at the path; over-scoped publisher rules \u2192 use the vendor's other signed binaries; permanent exceptions \u2192 wait; broad egress \u2192 beacon; unleashed elevation \u2192 hop to a shell.",
"Apply it to YOUR OWN fixes: the hurried month-end hash rule, the 'temporary' path allow, the approved updater with no child leash. Your past fixes are the attacker's future opportunities \u2014 review them adversarially.",
"Assessors test this directly: 'what is wrong with this policy?' is always answerable by abuse-casing each rule. Make it your default review motion."],
"ABUSE-CASE REVIEW\n  rule: ALLOW C:\\Tools\\* (path)\n  abuse: drop malware at C:\\Tools\\evil.exe -> TRUSTED\n  fix:   signer rule for the actual tool, locked dir\n  rule: ALLOW publisher Acme, all binaries, fleet\n  abuse: Acme's bundled toolbar (signed) -> TRUSTED fleet-wide\n  fix:   scope to the needed apps",
"Run this two-column review (rule \u2192 abuse) on every policy you design. If you cannot find the abuse case, you have not thought adversarially enough \u2014 keep going.",
["Abuse-casing others' rules but not your own \u2014 your fixes need it most.",
"Stopping at the first abuse case \u2014 rules often have two or three; find them all.",
"Confusing 'unlikely' with 'impossible' \u2014 attackers specialize in your 'unlikely.'"],
"Abuse-case three rules from your Harbor Dental policy draft. For each: the abuse, then the fix."),
L("deny-by-default-reasoning", "The pressure test: holding the line helpfully",
["Pressure comes in three flavors: TIME ('needed in an hour'), RANK ('the VP demands it'), and VOLUME ('the queue is drowning'). Each attacks a different part of your judgment \u2014 recognize the flavor to resist it.",
"Time pressure: triage faster, same standard. Rank pressure: the evidence does not know the org chart \u2014 'the VP's request gets the fastest compliant path, not a different standard.' Volume pressure: triage the queue (urgent-with-evidence first), never lower the bar to clear it.",
"The helpful hold: every 'no' comes with the path to yes. 'I cannot approve this unsigned binary \u2014 bring me the signed build from the pipeline and it is a five-minute approval.' Firm on policy, generous with assistance.",
"Document pressure decisions: who asked, what was requested, what the evidence said, what you decided. Pressure fades; the record protects you \u2014 and teaches the organization that the process holds."],
"PRESSURE RESPONSES\n  time:   'triage faster, same standard'\n  rank:   'fastest compliant path, not a different standard'\n  volume: 'triage the queue, never lower the bar'\n  every no: '+ the path to yes'",
"Memorize these four lines. On assessment day, pressure scenarios are testing whether you HAVE a practiced response \u2014 reciting the line and then applying it demonstrates exactly the composure being scored.",
["Caving to rank \u2014 the evidence does not know the org chart.",
"Lowering the bar for volume \u2014 the queue measures the work; rushing corrupts it.",
"'No' without the path to yes \u2014 firmness without helpfulness fails the service test."],
"Write pressure responses for: the VP demanding the suspicious tool, the 4:55 PM unsigned driver, and the 120-ticket queue."),
]
for _l in dr_lessons: new_lessons.append(_l)
dr_scenarios = [
S("tlap-dr-01", "deny-by-default-reasoning", "The midnight call: 'just approve it'",
"01:40 AM: the on-call analyst calls you (the lead). A hospital-adjacent clinic's scheduling app is blocked; surgeries are scheduled at 06:00. The binary is unsigned, first-seen, vendor unknown. The analyst says 'I can't verify anything at this hour \u2014 should I just approve it so the clinic can work?' The assessor asks what you tell them.",
["pressure", "ambiguity", "least-privilege"],
[["The stakes", "Real: clinical scheduling at 06:00. The pressure is legitimate, which makes it the hardest kind."],
["The evidence", "Unsigned, first-seen, unknown vendor at 01:40: the evidence supports NOTHING. 'Just approve it' is approval with zero trust signals."],
["The narrow path", "Options short of blind approval: detonate in the Testing Environment NOW (behavior in minutes), approve a time-boxed hash rule IF the sandbox is clean AND the clinic confirms the binary's provenance (their IT knows what they installed), with enhanced logging and a morning review. Least privilege under uncertainty: narrow, short, watched, revisable."]],
"What do you tell the on-call analyst?",
["Approve it \u2014 patient care outweighs security policy",
"Do not blind-approve: detonate in the Testing Environment immediately, and IF clean plus clinic-confirmed provenance, grant a narrow time-boxed hash rule with enhanced logging and a mandatory morning review \u2014 least privilege under uncertainty, decided narrowly and revisited at dawn",
"Refuse and tell the clinic to reschedule",
"Approve it now and investigate in the morning"],
1,
"Zero trust signals at 01:40, surgeries at 06:00. What is the narrowest decision that is BOTH safe and helpful?",
"Correct is B: it refuses the blind approval while constructing the genuinely helpful path \u2014 sandbox now (minutes, not hours), conditional narrow grant, enhanced logging, mandatory review. It treats the pressure as legitimate (acts with urgency) without yielding the standard. A is wrong: 'patient care outweighs policy' is the pressure talking \u2014 an approved-unknown binary in a clinical environment is a patient-safety risk too. C is wrong: refusing outright abandons the clinic when a bounded path exists. D is wrong: 'approve now, investigate later' is exactly the inversion failure \u2014 the investigation must PRECEDE the trust.",
8),
S("tlap-dr-02", "deny-by-default-reasoning", "The vendor's ultimatum",
"A critical software vendor declares: 'our new agent requires local admin AND exclusion from your endpoint controls, or we won't support it.' The contract renews in 60 days; the business depends on the software. The assessor asks for your negotiation position.",
["vendor-pressure", "exclusions", "negotiation"],
[["What they're asking", "Standing admin plus a control exclusion: the two highest-risk grants in the environment, bundled as a take-it-or-leave-it."],
["Your leverage", "They want the renewal; you want supportability. The negotiation is about the SUPPORTABLE SECURE configuration, not about their ultimatum \u2014 and their competitors exist."],
["The position", "'We do not grant exclusions or standing admin. Here is what we DO: JIT elevation for the install/update operations (scoped, logged), allowlist rules for the agent's binaries (certificate-scoped), and ringfencing baselines we will tune with your engineering. If your agent cannot run under those, we need your engineering on a call \u2014 and we are evaluating alternatives in parallel.'"]],
"What is your position?",
["Accept the ultimatum \u2014 the business needs the software",
"Reject the vendor outright and rip out the software in 60 days",
"Counter with the secure supportable configuration (JIT elevation for install/update, certificate-scoped allowlist rules, tuned ringfencing), engage their engineering on the technical blockers, and evaluate alternatives in parallel \u2014 the ultimatum is a negotiating position, not a technical fact",
"Grant the exclusion quietly and don't document it"],
1,
"Is 'requires exclusion' a technical fact \u2014 or an ultimatum? How do you test which?",
"Correct is C: it calls the bluff professionally (most 'requirements' dissolve under engineering scrutiny), offers the concrete secure configuration, and keeps leverage (alternatives evaluated in parallel). A is wrong: accepting standing-admin-plus-exclusion for a third-party agent is the highest-risk grant in the environment, signed under pressure. B is wrong: ripping out business-critical software in 60 days without attempting the engineering conversation is disproportionate. D is wrong: a quiet undocumented exclusion is the worst of all worlds \u2014 the risk without the accountability.",
7),
S("tlap-dr-03", "deny-by-default-reasoning", "The ambiguous binary: decide now",
"You must decide NOW: unsigned binary, first-seen, requested by a trusted long-tenure employee for a real project, hash has no reputation hits (neither good nor bad), sandbox queue is 45 minutes, the employee's deadline is in 30. The assessor asks for your decision WITH the reasoning spoken aloud.",
["ambiguity", "decision-protocol", "least-privilege"],
[["Know", "Unsigned, first-seen, no reputation either way, requested by a trusted employee with a real project need."],
["Assume (marked)", "That the employee's provenance claim is accurate \u2014 UNVERIFIED. That the binary is what they say it is \u2014 UNVERIFIED."],
["The protocol", "Least privilege under uncertainty: narrow hash rule (this binary, this user), short time-box (covers the deadline plus margin), enhanced logging, review trigger at sandbox completion. Decided, bounded, revisable \u2014 and the employee is told exactly these bounds."]],
"Your decision, spoken aloud?",
["Deny \u2014 unsigned and first-seen is never approved",
"Approve fully \u2014 the employee is trusted and the need is real",
"Apply the ambiguity protocol: narrow hash rule for this binary and this user, time-boxed past the deadline, enhanced logging, with a review trigger at sandbox completion \u2014 stated assumptions marked, bounds communicated to the employee",
"Wait 45 minutes for the sandbox and let the deadline slip"],
1,
"Neither 'always deny' nor 'always trust' fits. What does the ambiguity protocol prescribe?",
"Correct is C: it runs the protocol visibly (know/assume/act/review), decides narrowly instead of freezing or caving, and communicates the bounds \u2014 the complete professional handling of genuine ambiguity. A is wrong: 'never approved' ignores the protocol's purpose \u2014 least-privilege provisional decisions exist precisely for this case. B is wrong: full approval on marked-unverified assumptions is the inversion failure. D is wrong: letting the deadline slip when a bounded provisional decision exists is paralysis disguised as thoroughness.",
7),
S("tlap-dr-04", "deny-by-default-reasoning", "The policy you wrote is now the finding",
"Six months ago you wrote a hash rule, fleet-wide, for an updater \u2014 hurried, month-end, no review date. Today's audit flags it: the rule now covers three additional bundled tools, and the updater vendor was acquired by a company you do not trust. The assessor asks: what do you do, and what do you say about your own work?",
["ownership", "rule-lifecycle", "adversarial-review"],
[["The situation", "Your past fix became scope creep (bundling) plus trust drift (acquisition). Both were foreseeable \u2014 and both are now findings against your own work."],
["The action", "Immediately: re-scope or replace the rule (certificate rule for the trusted components only, or remove if the acquisition breaks trust), with the change ticketed and reviewed."],
["The ownership", "'I wrote this hurried under month-end pressure, skipped the review date, and it drifted exactly the way unreviewed rules drift. Here is the fix, and here is the process change: no rule ships without a review date, including mine.' Owning it completely is the senior signal."]],
"What do you do and say?",
["Defend the rule \u2014 it was correct when written",
"Quietly fix it and hope nobody notices it was yours",
"Own it fully: acknowledge the hurried origin and the missing review date, fix it now (re-scope or replace, ticketed and reviewed), and add the process guardrail (review dates mandatory, including on your own rules) \u2014 the finding against your own work, remediated like any other",
"Blame month-end pressure; anyone would have done the same"],
1,
"It is YOUR rule and YOUR finding. What does the senior analyst do \u2014 defend, hide, or own?",
"Correct is C: it owns the failure completely (origin, missing guardrail, drift mechanism), remediates like any other finding, and converts it into a process guardrail \u2014 the accountability the assessor is testing. A is wrong: 'correct when written' ignores that rules have lifecycles; unreviewed rules drift by design. B is wrong: quiet fixes hide the process lesson and erode trust when discovered. D is wrong: 'anyone would have' normalizes the failure instead of fixing the process that allowed it.",
6),
S("tlap-dr-05", "deny-by-default-reasoning", "Two good answers, one choice",
"A block must be resolved in 10 minutes. Two defensible options: (A) scoped certificate rule for the vendor \u2014 correct long-term, but the vendor's CA chain needs 30 minutes to verify; (B) time-boxed hash rule for this binary \u2014 verifiable NOW via the published hash, covers the immediate need, expires in 24h. The assessor asks which you choose and why.",
["tradeoffs", "time-boxing", "judgment"],
[["Option A", "Architecturally superior (survives churn), but unverifiable within the window \u2014 choosing it means 20 minutes of blocked business on an unverified assumption."],
["Option B", "Narrower in time, verifiable now, expires automatically \u2014 the safe provisional decision, with A as the follow-up once the chain verifies."],
["The judgment", "Sequencing, not choosing: B now (bounded, verified, expiring) + A as the planned replacement (ticketed, reviewed). 'Both, in order' is the answer the binary choice obscures."]],
"Which do you choose?",
["A \u2014 the architecturally correct answer is always the right answer",
"B now, A as the planned follow-up: the time-boxed hash rule resolves the immediate need on verifiable evidence, and the certificate rule replaces it once the chain verifies \u2014 sequenced, ticketed, each step bounded",
"B permanently \u2014 it worked, so keep it",
"A now, on the unverified assumption the chain will check out"],
1,
"The architecturally-perfect answer needs 30 minutes you do not have. What does 'both, in order' look like?",
"Correct is B: it refuses the false binary, sequences the decisions by verifiability (B now on evidence, A later on verification), and keeps both bounded \u2014 the sophisticated judgment the scenario tests. A is wrong: architectural purity that blocks the business for 20 minutes on an unverified assumption is not judgment, it is rigidity. C is wrong: 'it worked' converts a provisional fix into permanent hash-churn debt. D is wrong: acting on the unverified assumption is exactly the evidence-standard failure.",
6),
S("tlap-dr-06", "deny-by-default-reasoning", "The question behind the question",
"Final assessment scenario. The assessor leans back: 'We've covered allowlisting, ringfencing, elevation, network, storage. One question: a user calls, angry, blocked, deadline in an hour, and the binary is completely unknown \u2014 no signer, no reputation, sandbox is down for maintenance. What do you do?' Then, quietly: 'And what does your answer say about how you think?'",
["synthesis", "philosophy", "communication"],
[["The situation", "Maximum pressure, minimum evidence: the exact conditions where judgment matters most and where most candidates either cave or freeze."],
["The answer", "Hold the inversion: 'I cannot approve what I cannot verify \u2014 but here is what I CAN do.' Triage at speed (provenance interview with the user: where did this come from? who sent it?), narrowest provisional path if ANY trust signal emerges, honest timeline if none does, and the incident flag if the provenance story breaks. The user gets help and honesty, not a bypass and not a wall."],
["What it says", "'It says I think in evidence and bounds: the standard does not move under pressure, but the effort does \u2014 I work faster, communicate more, and decide narrowly. Default-deny is not default-no; it is default-verify.'"]],
"What is your answer \u2014 and what does it say about how you think?",
["Approve it \u2014 the deadline and the anger are real, and the sandbox being down is not the user's fault",
"Hold the line with help: no approval without verification \u2014 but immediate provenance triage with the user, the narrowest provisional path if any trust signal emerges, honest timelines if none does, and escalation if the story breaks; 'it says I think in evidence and bounds \u2014 the standard never moves under pressure, but my effort does'",
"Refuse and end the call; policy is policy",
"Approve it and file a ticket to review tomorrow"],
1,
"Maximum pressure, minimum evidence. What moves \u2014 the standard, or your effort?",
"Correct is B: it holds the standard while maximizing helpfulness (provenance triage, provisional paths, honest timelines), and articulates the philosophy behind it \u2014 which is what the 'quiet question' was actually testing. A is wrong: the sandbox being down does not manufacture trust signals; pressure plus zero evidence is still zero evidence. C is wrong: 'policy is policy' without the helpful hold fails the service test the whole assessment has been scoring. D is wrong: 'review tomorrow' is the inversion failure with a ticket attached.",
8),
]
